{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check; git status --short; git diff --name-only; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_reg_file.py\ncores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv\ncores/bench/test/test_reg_file.py\n# Register-file validity reset\n\nImplemented the hypothesis in `rtl/reg_file.sv`: the 32-by-32 data array\nhas a single synchronous write process with no reset assignment. A separate\nresettable written bitmap makes every register read zero after each reset\nuntil its next accepted write. Data writes and bitmap updates accept only\n`!reset && w_en && w_addr != 0`.\n\nBoth asynchronous read ports retain the exact priority: x0, live WB bypass,\nwritten stored data, then zero. Bypass remains active during asserted reset;\nreset wins at the capture edge. The interface, nret=1, pipeline, forwarding,\ninstruction timing, arithmetic, memory ports and RVFI are unchanged.\n\nNo architectural deviations. The portable two-read, one-write array lets\nthe synthesis tool choose any required replication. No vendor primitives or\nattributes were added. The existing five register-file tests and assertions\nare retained, with four additional tests for repeated reset, isolated\nunwritten/stale words, disabled writes, both-port bypass before capture,\nreset/write conflicts and live x0 bypass. The write helper now drives on the\nfalling edge and waits for capture to settle before removing write enable.\n\nLocal validation:\n\n- Required Verilator lint: PASS, no warnings.\n- `pytest -v cores/bench/test/test_reg_file.py`: PASS (nine cocotb tests).\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`: PASS; selftest RVFI trace comparison\n  without stalls and with both bus stalls, plus canonical CoreMark CRC/UART\n  and bounds checks.\n- `run_coremark_ipc`: PASS, 10 iterations in exactly 4,471,643\n  marker-bracketed cycles (unchanged); all completion, bounds and CRC/UART\n  checks pass.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=8 bash formal/run_all.sh`:\n  PASS on the first attempt, 53 passed and 0 failed. Auto-selected the\n  existing nret=1 wrapper/config. No fixes or counterexamples.\n- `make timing TARGET=bench`: completed for placement option 0, 83.976 MHz,\n  4,889 logic cells, 1,276 registers, 32 LUT-RAM, 4 BSRAM, 2 DSP. Gowin\n  synthesis reports extraction of RAM for `regs`. The reported worst paths\n  cross the divider, ALU and decode forwarding. Ran the same existing\n  `gowin.build_all` flow for options 1 and 2 without repeating option 0;\n  both placed successfully at 85.497 MHz. Three-option median: 85.497 MHz.\n  Fitness at the verified unchanged cycle count: 191.20 iterations/sec.\n\nAll functional gates passed. The performance hypothesis did not meet its\nacceptance criteria: median Fmax is below 95.428 MHz and fitness is below\n213.405 iterations/sec. It also regresses against the supplied champion's\n91.231 MHz / 204.02 iterations/sec despite the small logic-area reduction.\nThe written-bit read mask can add operand-path delay and the placement\nchange affects the existing competing divider/ALU/forwarding paths; the\nreported critical paths do not establish the mask as the cause. The RTL\nimplementation is retained for the orchestrator's independent evaluation.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
