{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_63", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-001-r11s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261004-001-r11s0',\n 'title':'Complete narrow-operand MUL directly into its held result register',\n 'category':'micro_opt',\n 'motivation':'''The champion scores 204.02 iterations/sec at 91.231 MHz over 4,471,643 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md is absent. The micro_opt history contains divider carry/sign optimizations, dispatch predecode and forwarding qualification, but no operand-width specialization of multiplication. The current multiplier spends a launch edge capturing operands and two busy edges registering partial products and the final result, even for small operands. CoreMark's matrix routines multiply sign-extended 16-bit MATDAT values, so many general 32-bit MUL instructions have operands that fit one native FPGA multiplier. Scratch ALU carry/result-selection and divider selector changes have not improved the tested placement option; shortening common multiplication latency offers a direct cycles-per-iteration benefit instead of relying entirely on timing gains.''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Add one operand-width fast path inside mul_unit for ALU_MUL only. Keep the existing general partial-product multiplier, all MULH/MULHU/MULHSU behavior and the core's pipeline boundaries and result handshake. This is a local arithmetic specialization, with no new instruction, issue policy, predictor, cache or benchmark-specific detection.\n\nRecognize narrow_mul = (op == ALU_MUL) && ((a[31:16] == 0) || (&a[31:16])) && ((b[31:16] == 0) || (&b[31:16])). Each qualifying 32-bit operand represents an integer in [-65536,65535], exactly expressible as the signed 17-bit value {operand[31], operand[15:0]}. This includes both sign-extended signed halfwords and zero-extended unsigned halfwords, as well as negative values down to -65536. It does not require low bit 15 to equal bit 31. Compute a signed 17x17 product at an explicit 34-bit width and use its low 32 bits. Low-word multiplication is identical modulo 2\"'^32 regardless of whether the original RV32 operands are interpreted as signed or unsigned. The guard must be evaluated from the final registered EX architectural operands presented at accepted launch, with no PC, memory address, CoreMark marker or program-content condition.\n\nOn an accepted narrow launch, capture narrow_product[31:0] directly into the existing result register, set result_valid=1 and busy=0, and skip both busy clocks. There must be no combinational narrow-product bypass into decode or EX/MEM: the fast result and its metadata are registered on the launch edge. Keep the general launch and both busy phases exactly for every nonqualifying MUL and every high-word multiplication. Preserve reset, capture priority for simultaneous result_accept and a new start, refusal of starts during busy or an unaccepted pending result, and stable result_valid/result until acceptance. Internal arithmetic completion remains independent of older dmem backpressure.\n\nThe existing ex_stage already captures mul_meta_q on mul_start and consumes a registered mul_valid only when MEM can advance. Use that protocol unchanged: metadata and fast result become available on the same edge; the following result-transfer edge releases the held instruction and forwards the exact result to younger decode. Preserve MUL-over-DIV completion priority and bubble/drain behavior, with no replay, duplicate register write or repeated store. Do not add early decode launch or an arithmetic path through the younger forwarding resolver.\n\nUnder RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, use the identical narrow predicate and completion schedule but capture the existing ALU_MUL launch_result formula on the fast edge; preserve all existing formulas and the slow ALTOPS schedule for other cases. The real 17x17 product belongs only in the real arithmetic branch. Implement explicit signed casts and widths so 0x0000ffff and 0xffff0000 represent +65535 and -65536 respectively.\n\nThis removes two multiplier wait clocks per qualifying MUL. The projected gain is eight percent with low confidence pending whole-core timing and measured fast-path incidence. At unchanged Fmax, clearing the 4.6 percent acceptance margin requires fewer than about 4,275,000 measured cycles; an Fmax change must be combined with the observed cycle count rather than assumed. The principal risks are the DSP-to-result launch path reducing Fmax, extra DSP/mux area perturbing placement, and insufficient narrow MUL frequency. Inspect Gowin mapping of the 17x17 product and use the median over all placement options. Keep only this fast path in the submitted implementation; scratch alternatives are not part of the hypothesis.\n\nPreserve the nret=1 IO/RVFI contract, exact retirement order and operands, all trap/decoder rules, full RV32M results, memory bounds and unchanged evaluation contracts. Retain every existing arithmetic assertion. Update only latency and partial-product-state expectations to distinguish the exactly specified fast and slow paths. Check all sign combinations, eligibility boundaries, fallback cases, mixed high/low operations, input poisoning, pending-result backpressure, reset, and simultaneous accept/start. Run real multiplier tests, pipeline dependence/stall tests, unchanged formal and cosim gates, canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/marker/bounds checks, and median Gowin timing before accepting a gain.'\"''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'},\n 'changes':[\n  {'file':'rtl/mul_unit.sv','description':'''Add the exact top-halfword eligibility predicate, explicit signed 17-bit operands and 34-bit native product for low-word MUL. At an accepted qualifying launch register the low product and publish a held result immediately, bypassing both busy phases. Preserve the existing general multiplier and all handshakes/reset/accept-start priorities. Mirror fast-path timing with the unchanged MUL formula under ALTOPS.'''} ,\n  {'file':'test/test_mul_unit.py','description':'''Preserve all arithmetic vectors and assertions. Distinguish fast launch completion from the original two busy clocks using an independent exact eligibility oracle; retain partial-product checks on the slow path. Add directed and deterministic random narrow products, guard boundaries around -65536/+65535 and just-outside operands, every sign combination, zero/all-one values, and high-word operations that must remain slow. Check stable completed data under poisoned inputs and attempted relaunches, mixed fast/slow transactions, reset and simultaneous accept/start.'''},\n  {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Add adjacent dependent narrow MULs, fast-to-slow and slow-to-fast multiplication sequences, high-word operations, load-produced operands, result-dependent ALU/branch/store work and x0 destinations. Check metadata/result association when a narrow MUL launches behind older stalled memory, and exactly-once acceptance/forwarding/retirement on release. Preserve exact RVFI operands, results, PCs, order, memory effects and progress bounds; adapt fixed multiplier-latency expectations only where the specified narrow path now completes sooner.'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,allow_unicode=True,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Revised schema-valid hypothesis:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
