only the initial normal-result\nforwarding qualification with\nforward_wen = in.valid && in.fast_write && !misalign_fault.\nKeep dynamic branch/JAL/JALR misalignment suppression in EX. Retain the\nexisting subsequent mul_valid/div_valid overrides, their priority,\ndestination selection and completed-result data. Keep every original\nlaunch, busy, pending-result, memory-hold, acceptance, drain-bubble and\nredirect guard unchanged. Preserve the binary opcode connection to\nalu, mul_unit and div_unit, including exact ALTOPS behavior. No source\ntag comparison or forwarding-data selection is moved across a cycle.\n\nFor every valid instruction, the new normal-forwarding expression is\nalgebraica\n…[27874 of 30946 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:70183db7ce34a568]…\n percent higher fitness with low confidence: this exact candidate has not been vendor-measured, and EX forwarding, branch correction or divider paths may still determine Fmax. Do not claim the unsuccessful scratch arithmetic/branch variants as evidence of a gain for this proposal. At unchanged cycles, acceptance requires more than 230.643 iterations/sec, approximately 97.159 MHz median Gowin Fmax. Measure all scored placement options and unchanged marker-bracketed cycles; a smaller mux count alone is insufficient.\n\nKeep nret=1 and the entire core IO/RVFI contract, default-illegal decoding, all trap rules, exact real RV32M and ALTOPS results, strict retirement order, arbitrary memory backpressure, forward progress, memory bounds and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/marker invariants. Retain every existing correctness assertion and run standard formal, arithmetic tests, trace cosim, canonical CoreMark checks and median vendor timing. Scratch files are observations only and must not become build dependencies.\nNOTES \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
