{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_46", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-001-r4s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261004-001-r4s0\ntitle: \"Shorten the single-cycle multiplier with DSP-sized partial products\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 107.83 iter/sec at 48.433 MHz with 4,491,485\n  marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its worst reported path crosses\n  forwarding and the ordinary execute ALU before reaching data memory,\n  taking 25.997 ns and 17 cells. The radix-16 alternative also exposed\n  this ALU path and scored 105.02, whereas the previous micro_opt radix-4\n  divider change improved fitness by 9.63 percent. No LESSONS.md exists.\n  The ALU still expresses three wide signed/unsigned products selected\n  into its common result. A local multiplier mapping improvement can\n  shorten this newly exposed path without increasing instruction cycles.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the three full-width multiplication expressions in alu.sv\n  with a single shared combinational partial-product implementation of\n  MUL, MULH, MULHU and MULHSU, sized for the FPGA's 18-bit DSP inputs.\n  Keep the existing op/a/b/out interface and zero-cycle ALU latency.\n  This is one arithmetic mapping optimization, with no pipeline, divider,\n  branch-prediction, cache or bus-protocol changes.\n\n  Split each operand into 16-bit low and high halves. Interpret the high\n  half of a as signed only for MULH/MULHSU, and the high half of b as\n  signed only for MULH. Represent each high half as a signed 17-bit value\n  with its selected sign extension; represent low halves as nonnegative\n  signed 17-bit values. Compute the four low-low, low-high, high-low and\n  high-high products independently, with explicitly sized results, so\n  each product fits a native DSP multiplier. Compute the complete product\n  modulo 2^64 as P00 + (P01 << 16) + (P10 << 16) + (P11 << 32).\n  Sign-extend cross products before shifting; retain sufficient bits for\n  unsigned high halves up to 65535 and signed high halves down to -32768.\n  The high-high term may be truncated modulo 2^32 before its shift.\n\n  Recombine the four terms with explicit carry-save compression and \n…[1901 of 4973 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d9635bf73d77eb6f]…\n      and exact ALTOPS formulas. Keep widths and signed casts explicit.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all existing arithmetic assertions. Add deterministic\n      differential vectors for all four multiply operations, every sign\n      combination, zero, INT_MIN, INT_MAX, UINT_MAX, half-word boundaries,\n      cross-term carries and high-half overflow. Compare against exact\n      Python integer products masked to 64 bits and select the requested\n      half; exercise changes between signed and unsigned operations.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain existing checks and cover mixed multiply operations with\n      EX/MEM and MEM/WB operand forwarding, dependent multiply chains,\n      load-to-multiply, multiplication results used as load/store\n      addresses and store data, x0 destinations and both bus stalls.\n      Assert exact RVFI operands/results/order and unchanged instruction\n      latency without weakening trap, progress or memory-side-effect checks.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
