{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-001-r5s0.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-001-r5s0\ntitle: \"Shorten integer addition with parallel carry-select blocks\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion achieves 125.68 iterations/sec at 60.0 MHz with 4,773,913\n  marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path starts at an\n  EX/MEM destination tag, crosses forwarding and the integer ALU, and\n  reaches the memory interface in 22.864 ns. Multiplication and division\n  now have registered execution units. No LESSONS.md exists. Earlier\n  micro_opt forwarding retiming improved timing on the previous core,\n  but did not remove arithmetic delay; no recorded attempt has optimized\n  the fast integer adder itself. Reducing carry propagation after the\n  operand muxes can improve every instruction's clock without adding cycles.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the fast ALU's 32-bit addition with an explicitly partitioned\n  carry-select adder, keeping the existing combinational op/a/b/out\n  interface and every operation's architectural result. Use four 8-bit\n  blocks. The low block computes its sum and carry once. Each upper block\n  independently computes 9-bit zero-extended sums for carry-in zero and\n  carry-in one, in parallel with the low block. Select each upper sum\n  using the incoming block carry. Compute block carries with a balanced\n  prefix over block generate and propagate rather than a serial chain\n  of three carry-selection multiplexers. Derive generate from the\n  zero-carry sum's ninth bit and propagate from reduction-AND of the\n  block's bitwise operand XOR. Keep all arithmetic widths explicit.\n\n  Apply this mapping to ALU_ADD only, leaving ALU_SUB, comparisons,\n  shifts, Boolean operations and LUI unchanged. Keep this local to the\n  integer ALU: pipeline stages, forwarding priority, memory protocols,\n  branch resolution, multiplier/divider arithmetic and execution\n  schedules remain unchanged. Do not share addition with subtraction\n  through an opcode-controlled operand inversion; that would put an\n  additional control mux before the address addition. The objective is\n  to shorten the post-forwarding ADD carry path, which also computes\n  load/store effective addresses, without adding a pipeline register.\n\n  Preserve nret=1 and every IO/RVFI field, decoder legality, traps,\n  retirement order, early divider launch, ALTOPS substitutions and\n  arbitrary imem/dmem backpressure. Require bit-exact modulo-2^32 ADD\n  results for carry propagation across every block boundary and signed\n  overflow. Validate the unchanged functional contract with the existing\n  unit, formal and cosim gates and canonical CoreMark CRC/marker checks.\n  Confirm median Gowin Fmax and marker cycles. At unchanged cycles,\n  acceptance requires more than 62.76 MHz, or fitness above 131.46128.\n  The estimate is a ten-percent fitness gain; FPGA mapping, extra local\n  sum muxes and a different limiting path are the principal uncertainties.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement the ALU_ADD result with four explicit 8-bit carry-select\n      blocks, parallel carry-zero/carry-one candidate sums, balanced\n      block carry prefix and exact 32-bit wraparound. Preserve the ALU\n      interface, combinational latency and every other operation.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Retain every existing assertion. Add deterministic differential\n      ADD vectors and directed carries across bits 8, 16 and 24,\n      full-width wraparound, mixed block generate/propagate/kill patterns,\n      signed extrema and back-to-back operand/op changes. Check exact\n      32-bit results against integer addition masked to 0xffffffff.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
