{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_32", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-001-r6s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261004-001-r6s0\ntitle: \\\"Retime divider sign conversion across its existing register boundaries\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 132.63 iterations/second at 63.318 MHz with\n  4,773,913 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path\n  runs from an ID/EX destination tag through decode forwarding into\n  div_unit.divisor_q, which captures a conditionally negated 32-bit\n  operand. The previous one-hot forwarding experiment exposed a\n  divider-to-signed-result path instead. Both paths append sign\n  conversion to another substantial combinational path. Earlier\n  micro_opt attempts addressed forwarding, launch qualification and\n  radix-4 recurrence; this proposal uses the existing divider state\n  boundaries to separate sign conversion from those critical paths.\n  LESSONS.md is absent; the micro_opt history and latest champion in\n  log.jsonl were reviewed before proposing this change.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retime only the real divider's input and output sign conversions.\n  Keep its interface, early decode launch, eight busy clocks, two\n  radix-4 digits per busy clock, and start/busy/result-valid/accept\n  protocol unchanged. This is a local combinational logic retiming\n  within the existing execution unit, with no new pipeline stage or\n  additional instruction cycle.\n\n  At accepted launch, capture raw a into quotient_q and raw b into\n  divisor_q instead of computing their magnitudes before those\n  registers. Keep the existing remainder_negative_q = signed_op &&\n  a[31], quotient_negative_q = signed_op && (a[31] XOR b[31]) &&\n  (b \"'!= 0), and select_remainder_q. Add one divisor_negative_q flag\n  capturing signed_op && b[31]. These sign/control captures are\n  independent of the full-width magnitude computation.\n\n  During busy group zero, compute initial_a = remainder_negative_q\n  ? (0 - quotient_q) : quotient_q and initial_b = divisor_negative_q\n  ? (0 - divisor_q) : divisor_q, all at 32-bit width. Apply the existing\n  narrow initial radix-4 logic to initial_a'\"'s top nibble and initial_b's\n  magnitude, including the D >= 16 bypass. Specifically, initial D/2D/3D\n  use initial_b[3:0], both trial digits use initial_a[31:28], and the\n  bypass predicate is OR(initial_b[31:4]). Capture quotient_q =\n  {initial_a[27:0], quotient_nibble}, remainder_q =\n  {28'b0, remainder_nibble}, divisor_q = initial_b, and divisor_3x_q =\n  {2'b0,initial_b} + {1'b0,initial_b,1'b0} on that same group-zero edge.\n  Use explicit 34-bit widths for 3D. Groups one through seven then\n  execute the current full-width recurrence without changes. The\n  dividend's remaining bits must come from initial_a, not the raw\n  launch word. No normalization/preparation clock is added.\n\n  On group seven, register the selected unsigned q2/r2 magnitude in\n  result_magnitude_q instead of performing sign correction before\n  result capture. Drive the existing result port combinationally as\n  the conditional negation of this registered magnitude using the\n  existing captured quotient/remainder sign selection. Do not add a\n  busy/valid/start-controlled output-data mux. The architectural\n  result is available in the same result-valid cycle as before and\n  reaches EX/MEM on the same acceptance edge. Its magnitude and sign\n  flags must remain stable while result_valid is pending; a new\n  accepted start may replace sign flags only when the old result is\n  absent or simultaneously accepted. Reset the new flag and magnitude\n  register to zero, preserving reset cancellation and accept/start\n  overlap. Keep raw RVFI operand/metadata capture in EX unchanged.\n\n  Leave RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas and their original registered\n  result/latency exactly unchanged. Confine all magnitude/sign\n  retiming to the real-arithmetic branch. Preserve signed truncation\n  toward zero, dividend-signed remainders, unsigned high-bit inputs,\n  INT_MIN/-1 and divide-by-zero quotient/remainder behavior. In\n  particular, retain quotient sign suppression for b=0: the natural\n  all-ones quotient must never be negated.\n\n  This removes the wide negate from forwarded decode-to-launch\n  capture and from the final two-digit recurrence-to-result capture.\n  Input normalization instead shares the existing narrow first group;\n  output correction runs from an already registered result. A scratch\n  prototype of this exact change passed the existing real divider and\n  complete pipeline cocotb suites, including eight-clock divider and\n  ten-cycle ready-bus retirement-gap checks. The unchanged Gowin flow\n  with the nret=1 FPGA wrapper measured 74.883/75.375/75.375 MHz for\n  placement options 0/1/2, median 75.375 MHz. It used 3,719 logic cells\n  and 805 registers, versus 3,704 and 804 in the champion, with unchanged\n  LUT-RAM/BSRAM/DSP counts. Its worst median-placement path was\n  18.561 ns inside the divider rather than sign conversion appended\n  to the early launch path. These are prototype measurements, not\n  claims of an orchestrator fitness result.\n\n  At the champion's cycle count, that median projects approximately\n  157.89 iterations/second, a 19.04-percent gain. Expect nineteen\n  percent higher fitness with medium confidence. The main risks are\n  group-zero negate-plus-3D timing, result correction feeding younger\n  decode/early launch, and exact preservation of signed corner cases\n  and pending-result stability. Acceptance at unchanged cycles requires\n  median Fmax above approximately 66.23 MHz. Preserve nret=1, all\n  external ports, decode defaults, RVFI/order/trap rules, arbitrary\n  bus waits, bounded progress and every evaluation invariant. Require\n  existing formal, real-arithmetic, trace cosim and canonical CoreMark\n  CRC/UART/marker checks and reproduce the median vendor timing.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 19\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/div_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture raw operands and one added divisor sign flag at launch.\n      Move both magnitude conversions into the existing narrow group\n      zero and register normalized D plus full-width 3D on its edge.\n      Keep groups one through seven unchanged. Register unsigned final\n      magnitude, then conditionally sign-correct the result port from\n      stable captured flags. Preserve all handshakes, latency, reset,\n      corner cases and the untouched ALTOPS arithmetic/protocol.\n  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all original arithmetic and exact eight-clock handshake\n      assertions. Add signed group-zero state checks using raw capture\n      followed by magnitude-derived quotient/remainder and 34-bit 3D,\n      especially negative divisors with magnitude 0..17, INT_MIN and\n      high-bit unsigned inputs. Verify held result stability while\n      live operands/op/start change, both sign combinations, divide\n      by zero, reset cancellation and simultaneous accept/new start.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain the existing pipeline suite and retirement-gap bounds.\n      Exercise immediate consumers of signed DIV/REM results through\n      completion bypass, empty-EX early division, negative load-to-div\n      dependencies and completion under data-memory backpressure.\n      Check exact RVFI operands/results/order and exactly-once stores.\nYAML\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_32", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-001-r6s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261004-001-r6s0\ntitle: \\\"Retime divider sign conversion across its existing register boundaries\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 132.63 iterations/second at 63.318 MHz with\n  4,773,913 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path\n  runs from an ID/EX destination tag through decode forwarding into\n  div_unit.divisor_q, which captures a conditionally negated 32-bit\n  operand. The previous one-hot forwarding experiment exposed a\n  divider-to-signed-result path instead. Both paths append sign\n  conversion to another substantial combinational path. Earlier\n  micro_opt attempts addressed forwarding, launch qualification and\n  radix-4 recurrence; this proposal uses the existing divider state\n  boundaries to separate sign conversion from those critical paths.\n  LESSONS.md is absent; the micro_opt history and latest champion in\n  log.jsonl were reviewed before proposing this change.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retime only the real divider's input and output sign conversions.\n  Keep its interface, early decode launch, eight busy clocks, two\n  radix-4 digits per busy clock, and start/busy/result-valid/accept\n  protocol unchanged. This is a local combinational logic retiming\n  within the existing execution unit, with no new pipeline stage or\n  additional instruction cycle.\n\n  At accepted launch, capture raw a into quotient_q and raw b into\n  divisor_q instead of computing their magnitudes before those\n  registers. Keep the existing remainder_negative_q = signed_op &&\n  a[31], quotient_negative_q = signed_op && (a[31] XOR b[31]) &&\n  (b \"'!= 0), and select_remainder_q. Add one divisor_negative_q flag\n  capturing signed_op && b[31]. These sign/control captures are\n  independent of the full-width magnitude computation.\n\n  During busy group zero, compute initial_a = remainder_negative_q\n  ? (0 - quotient_q) : quotient_q and initial_b = divisor_negative_q\n  ? (0 - divisor_q) : divisor_q, all at 32-bit width. Apply the existing\n  narrow initial radix-4 logic to initial_a'\"'s top nibble and initial_b's\n  magnitude, including the D >= 16 bypass. Specifically, initial D/2D/3D\n  use initial_b[3:0], both trial digits use initial_a[31:28], and the\n  bypass predicate is OR(initial_b[31:4]). Capture quotient_q =\n  {initial_a[27:0], quotient_nibble}, remainder_q =\n  {28'b0, remainder_nibble}, divisor_q = initial_b, and divisor_3x_q =\n  {2'b0,initial_b} + {1'b0,initial_b,1'b0} on that same group-zero edge.\n  Use explicit 34-bit widths for 3D. Groups one through seven then\n  execute the current full-width recurrence without changes. The\n  dividend's remaining bits must come from initial_a, not the raw\n  launch word. No normalization/preparation clock is added.\n\n  On group seven, register the selected unsigned q2/r2 magnitude in\n  result_magnitude_q instead of performing sign correction before\n  result capture. Drive the existing result port combinationally as\n  the conditional negation of this registered magnitude using the\n  existing captured quotient/remainder sign selection. Do not add a\n  busy/valid/start-controlled output-data mux. The architectural\n  result is available in the same result-valid cycle as before and\n  reaches EX/MEM on the same acceptance edge. Its magnitude and sign\n  flags must remain stable while result_valid is pending; a new\n  accepted start may replace sign flags only when the old result is\n  absent or simultaneously accepted. Reset the new flag and magnitude\n  register to zero, preserving reset cancellation and accept/start\n  overlap. Keep raw RVFI operand/metadata capture in EX unchanged.\n\n  Leave RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas and their original registered\n  result/latency exactly unchanged. Confine all magnitude/sign\n  retiming to the real-arithmetic branch. Preserve signed truncation\n  toward zero, dividend-signed remainders, unsigned high-bit inputs,\n  INT_MIN/-1 and divide-by-zero quotient/remainder behavior. In\n  particular, retain quotient sign suppression for b=0: the natural\n  all-ones quotient must never be negated.\n\n  This removes the wide negate from forwarded decode-to-launch\n  capture and from the final two-digit recurrence-to-result capture.\n  Input normalization instead shares the existing narrow first group;\n  output correction runs from an already registered result. A scratch\n  prototype of this exact change passed the existing real divider and\n  complete pipeline cocotb suites, including eight-clock divider and\n  ten-cycle ready-bus retirement-gap checks. The unchanged Gowin flow\n  with the nret=1 FPGA wrapper measured 74.883/75.375/75.375 MHz for\n  placement options 0/1/2, median 75.375 MHz. It used 3,719 logic cells\n  and 805 registers, versus 3,704 and 804 in the champion, with unchanged\n  LUT-RAM/BSRAM/DSP counts. Its worst median-placement path was\n  18.561 ns inside the divider rather than sign conversion appended\n  to the early launch path. These are prototype measurements, not\n  claims of an orchestrator fitness result.\n\n  At the champion's cycle count, that median projects approximately\n  157.89 iterations/second, a 19.04-percent gain. Expect nineteen\n  percent higher fitness with medium confidence. The main risks are\n  group-zero negate-plus-3D timing, result correction feeding younger\n  decode/early launch, and exact preservation of signed corner cases\n  and pending-result stability. Acceptance at unchanged cycles requires\n  median Fmax above approximately 66.23 MHz. Preserve nret=1, all\n  external ports, decode defaults, RVFI/order/trap rules, arbitrary\n  bus waits, bounded progress and every evaluation invariant. Require\n  existing formal, real-arithmetic, trace cosim and canonical CoreMark\n  CRC/UART/marker checks and reproduce the median v