{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_64", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-001-r8s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261004-001-r8s0\ntitle: \"Register one-hot execution controls before the EX result path\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion scores 174.53 iterations/sec at 83.321 MHz and\n  4,773,913 bracketed cycles. Its reported worst path starts at\n  ID/EX ctrl.alu_op[4] and crosses EX to the timed data-memory circuit.\n  EX repeatedly decodes the five-bit operation for the fast ALU, multiply\n  and divide dispatch, forwarding eligibility and pipeline draining.\n  LESSONS.md is absent. Previous micro_opt attempts improved divider\n  arithmetic and forwarding, but none predecoded the current registered\n  EX operation controls. With division now launched from registered EX,\n  shortening this remaining operation-control path can raise Fmax for\n  every instruction without spending additional CoreMark cycles.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move EX operation decoding across the existing ID/EX register. Prepare\n  eleven mutually exclusive fast-ALU result enables plus dedicated\n  multiply-class and divide-class bits in decode, and capture them with\n  the exact instruction and final operands. Keep the binary operation\n  field for the unchanged mul_unit/div_unit operation selection and\n  ALTOPS formulas. This is one local control encoding and retiming\n  optimization; preserve all pipeline boundaries and instruction schedules.\n\n  Drive the instantiated fast ALU from the registered one-hot enables.\n  Preserve each existing ADD, SUB, logical, signed/unsigned comparison,\n  shift and LUI computation, but select their results with independently\n  masked candidates and a balanced OR tree. Do not regenerate the enables\n  combinationally from the registered binary opcode inside EX. Keep ADD\n  and SUB arithmetic and all shift/sign semantics unchanged; introduce\n  no shared carry chain, new shifter algorithm or extra arithmetic state.\n  The captured multiplier/divider class bits replace the four-operation\n  equality networks used for is_mul/is_div in ex_stage. Apply in.valid\n  and all existing pending-result, stall and acceptance guards\n…[2645 of 5717 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8ef6e91320a71c64]…\np.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire captured fast enables to the ALU and use captured multiply/divide\n      class controls for dispatch and normal-result eligibility. Preserve\n      validity, misalignment, launch/accept guards, completion priority,\n      forwarding, memory holds, drain bubbles and both arithmetic schedules.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Adapt the standalone ALU driver to its one-hot control interface\n      without removing any assertion. Check every fast operation against\n      an integer reference, zero-enable bubbles, overflow, signed extrema,\n      all shift amounts and deterministic randomized operand pairs.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve existing architectural, arithmetic, trap, order, side-effect\n      and retirement-gap checks. Verify control/instruction association and\n      exactly-once dispatch across bubbles, redirects, memory stalls and\n      mixed MUL/DIV/REM/fast operations with dependent consumers.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
