{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_73","type":"agent_message","text":"The multiplier variants passed arithmetic and pipeline checks, but their timing losses outweighed the cycle savings. I’m replacing the draft with an unmeasured structural proposal: a small fall-through instruction queue that lets fetch run ahead during execute and memory holds. The empty-queue bypass preserves the current branch redirect latency."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_74", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r10s1.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261004-002-r10s1\ntitle: \\\"Decouple instruction fetch with a four-entry fall-through queue\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 192.27 iter/sec at 91.79 MHz and needs 4,773,913\n  marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its combinational IF/ID view couples\n  the fetch PC to every load-use, data-memory and arithmetic hold. During\n  those holds an otherwise ready instruction bus does no useful work;\n  after execution resumes, an unready instruction bus immediately inserts\n  another bubble. With roughly 22 percent independent stalls on each bus,\n  buffering instructions obtained during backend holds can reduce these\n  serial penalties. Prior structural address-lane and EX/MEM payload\n  changes did not improve the champion, and no LESSONS.md exists. Scratch\n  payload-control and faster-multiplier alternatives in this proposal\n  session also lost too much Gowin Fmax. Decoupling fetch attacks measured\n  cycle cost without extending the arithmetic datapaths.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the globally held fetch view with an autonomous sequential\n  fetch producer and a four-entry, fall-through instruction FIFO. Keep\n  the existing single-issue ID/EX, EX/MEM and MEM/WB boundaries, operand\n  forwarding and blocking arithmetic units. This is one structural\n  separation of frontend production from backend consumption; the FIFO\n  is neither a cache nor a predictor and contains only fetched instruction\n  words and their associated full-width PCs.\n\n  When the FIFO is empty, present the current raw bus instruction and\n  producer PC directly to decode. Accept it only when io_imemReady and\n  decode acceptance are both true. Consume directly without enqueueing\n  and advance the producer PC by four on that edge. This empty bypass is\n  essential: a compulsory registered IF/ID stage would add a bubble to\n  every taken control transfer and could erase the gain. Keep raw fetch\n  data visible for combinational decode independently of acceptance.\n\n  When decode is held and the instruction bus is ready, enqueue the\n  current producer instruction/PC if there is capacity, advancing the\n  producer PC even though ID/EX cannot advance. When the FIFO is nonempty,\n  decode sees its oldest instruction and PC and can accept that head\n  even with io_imemReady=0. Independently fetch/enqueue the producer word\n  on a ready bus whenever space exists, including space freed by a\n  simultaneous dequeue. The producer PC advances exactly once for each\n  accepted bus word, whether directly consumed or enqueued. Never enqueue\n  an unready response, lose a full-queue word, or enqueue the same response\n  twice. Full FIFO plus no dequeue holds the producer PC. A stopped bus\n  holds the producer address while buffered decode can continue.\n\n  Prefer a shallow four-word shift FIFO with a fixed registered head and\n  a single empty/head bypass mux, rather than an asynchronous indexed RAM\n  read or a late backend-ready mux on every instruction bit. Store PCs\n  explicitly, or use a registered head PC whose sequential increments\n  are proved to remain associated with the stored words; do not derive\n  the head PC through a full-width subtractor on the decode path. Use\n  registered occupancy and queue state to select the raw/head payload.\n  Redirect, flush, io_imemReady and arithmetic completion must qualify\n  acceptance/control rather than select or zero wide decode payloads.\n  Reset queue control and payload deterministically.\n\n  An accepted EX redirect has highest priority: clear all queued younger\n  words, suppress direct consumption/enqueue/dequeue on that edge and\n  load the producer PC with the exact existing redirect target. Flush the\n  currently exposed younger decode instruction with the existing rules.\n  Redirect must override an unready bus, a full queue and fetch holds;\n  a branch behind an older unready memory request must still wait for\n  the existing EX advance before redirecting. Do not bypass-fetch a new\n  target on the redirect edge. The next cycle uses the empty FIFO bypass,\n  preserving current ready-bus branch/JAL/JALR timing and all misalignment\n  trap/link-write behavior. Use full 32-bit modular PCs and existing bus\n  addresses; introduce no address truncation, clamping or safe-target\n  substitution. Wrong-path prefetched words never reach side effects.\n\n  Separate hazard meanings explicitly. Backend holds continue to govern\n  ID/EX, load-use bubble injection, EX/MEM and MEM/WB exactly as today,\n  but do not prohibit producer prefetch. Instruction availability for\n  the hazard unit becomes head_valid || io_imemReady; its lack suppresses\n  decode acceptance only when no buffered head exists. Load-use checks\n  use the exposed consumer instruction's source tags, never the producer\n  io_imemData tags. Keep the valid EX-load and nonzero-rd comparisons.\n  A load-use bubble retains the queue head while the producer may fill\n  spare entries. MUL/DIV waits likewise retain the oldest consumer and\n  permit bounded prefetch; older data requests and completed arithmetic\n  retain the existing hold/drain/accept protocol. Wire an explicit\n  consumer-accept handshake so a flush-over-hold load-use edge cannot\n  accidentally dequeue. Buffered acceptance is independent of instruction\n  bus readiness but still forbidden by every real backend hold.\n\n  Preserve nret=1 and every top-level bus/RVFI port, decoder default\n  illegality, traps, exact RV32M and ALTOPS behavior, architectural source\n  operands and PCs, strict retirement order, bounded memory accesses,\n  exactly-once stores and the unchanged formal liveness/progress checks.\n  Keep all arithmetic and architectural tests. Update only frontend\n  