{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_62", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r13s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261004-002-r13s1',\n'title':'Stream low-half MUL through EX and MEM instead of blocking execution',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion delivers 220.50 iterations/sec at median 92.886 MHz over\n4,212,505 bracketed CoreMark cycles. The existing multiplier blocks\nfetch/decode for operand capture, two busy clocks and completion\ntransfer. Previous reference profiling recorded about 93,960 MUL\ninstructions in canonical CoreMark. Removing two of their three extra\nexecution cycles could save roughly 187,920 cycles, about 4.46 percent\nof champion cycles before accounting for bus-stall overlap. Independent\nmultiply consumers offer further overlap. No LESSONS.md is present.\nEarlier narrow-operand immediate completion lost clock rate, so this\nproposal keeps DSP products between registers and changes their pipeline\nplacement rather than adding arithmetic to decode launch.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Give ALU_MUL, the RV32M low-half multiplication operation, a dedicated\nstreaming two-stage lane spanning the existing EX/MEM and MEM/WB\nboundaries. Keep MULH/MULHU/MULHSU in the existing blocking registered\nmultiplier and keep the divider, integer pipeline, agree predictor and\nsingle-entry fetch skid buffer unchanged. This is one structural change\nto the common low-half multiply's execution/forwarding schedule, with\nno extra ordinary-instruction pipeline stage.\n\nIn EX compute three unsigned 16-by-16 partial products directly from\nID/EX's already registered, fully resolved operands. Capture P00=Alo*Blo\nat 32 bits and the low sixteen bits of P01=Alo*Bhi and P10=Ahi*Blo at\nthe existing EX/MEM edge, alongside that instruction's normal retirement\nmetadata and a low_mul flag. There is no additional operand-capture\nregister or busy state for this operation. Use synthesizable product\nexpressions with explicit widths so Gowin can infer DSPs. Ahi/Bhi are\nunsigned bit slices; signedness has no effect on a product modulo 2\"'^32.\nNo high-by-high product is needed for low-half MUL.\n\nIn MEM compute upper16=(P00[31:16]+P01[15:0]+P10[15:0]) mod 2''^16\nwith a sixteen-bit carry-save compressor followed by one sixteen-bit\ncarry-propagate addition. The exact result is\n{upper16,P00[15:0]}. Capture it as the instruction'\"'s normal MEM/WB\nwriteback value and expose the same exact value on MEM forwarding.\nDo not append a 32-bit high-result adder or sign correction to this\nlane. Keep the full original raw operands in RVFI metadata.\n\nLow-half MUL has no EX forwarding candidate, just as a load has none.\nExtend the existing one-cycle load-use interlock to a valid EX low-half\nMUL with a nonzero destination matching either raw younger source tag.\nHold the fetch head and inject one ID/EX control bubble while the\nproducer advances. The consumer then captures the exact MEM multiply\nresult through the current MEM forwarding path. An independent younger\ninstruction advances immediately, and independent consecutive low-half\nMULs may launch on consecutive clocks. Keep EX-first/MEM-second/RF-third\nproducer priority, WB write-first bypass, x0 handling and captured\narchitectural operand semantics. A dependent MUL-to-MUL or MUL-to-branch,\nload/store, JALR or DIV consumer must obtain the exact result.\n\nExclude only ALU_MUL from the existing blocking multiplier's launch and\nwait predicates; retain its high-half operations, saved metadata,\nregistered completion, acceptance and forwarding. This prevents two\nowners of the same low-half instruction. A low-half MUL already in MEM\nmay drain while a younger high-half multiply or divide starts. A blocking\narithmetic completion may forward into a following low-half MUL normally.\nPreserve old EX/MEM arithmetic-bubble and completed-result priorities;\nan invalid bubble clears low_mul eligibility and cannot retire or issue\na memory request. Partial-product payload may remain harmless when invalid.\n\nAn older stalled load/store still freezes EX/MEM and the low-half\nproduct capture with its metadata. Hold the older memory request stable,\nthen capture the waiting multiply exactly once when MEM becomes ready.\nA low-half MUL in MEM is not a data-memory operation and cannot be held\nmerely because io_dmemReady is zero. Preserve MEM/WB clearing on genuine\ndata stalls, arithmetic drain semantics, wrong-path control kills and\nexactly-once architectural retirement. Reset cancels all valid lane work.\n\nUnder RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS capture the exact existing MUL formula\n(a+b)\"'^0x5876063e into the EX/MEM lane and return it through the same\nMEM forwarding/writeback selection and one-bubble dependency protocol.\nKeep every high-half multiplication and division ALTOPS formula and\nreal arithmetic implementation unchanged. Keep nret=1, external ports,\nall RVFI fields, legality and trap discipline, strict order, bounded\nmemory accesses and the existing formal depths/progress checks. Preserve\nall real arithmetic assertions, CRCs, timing markers and UART checks.\n\nProject seven percent fitness improvement with low confidence. With\n93,960 immediate dependent low-half multiplies and no stall overlap,\nremoving two extra cycles projects 4,024,585 cycles and about 230.8\niterations/sec at unchanged Fmax, only slightly above the acceptance\nthreshold. Independent instructions can improve that saving, but saved\ncycles may already overlap stalls. The short sixteen-bit final sum and\nunchanged ordinary EX forwarding are intended to retain clock rate;\nadditional DSP placement/routing and MEM-to-ID forwarding remain risks.\nThis streaming lane is unmeasured. Require actual marker-bracketed cycles\nand median Gowin Fmax to exceed 230.643 iterations/sec. At 4,024,585 cycles\nthis requires more than 92.821 MHz; do not assume the cycle projection\nor a timing gain. Keep the unchanged ten-cycle ready-bus retirement-gap\nchecks, full formal/cosim gates and every existing architectural assertion.'\"''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':7,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Extend ex_mem_t with low_mul eligibility and its 32-bit P00 and\nsixteen-bit low P01/P10 products. Preserve the full architectural\nmetadata and downstream RVFI contract.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/low_mul.sv','description':'''Add the dedicated low-half product lane: explicit unsigned halfword\nproducts for EX capture and an exact sixteen-bit CSA plus final sum\nfor MEM assembly. Include the unchanged MUL ALTOPS expression using\nthe same pipeline boundary; introduce no vendor-only primitive or\nexternal initialization dependency.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Capture low-half MUL partial products with normal EX/MEM metadata\nunder the existing hold/reset/flush/accept priorities. Exclude this\noperation from blocking mul_start/mul_wait and EX forwarding while\nretaining high-half multiplication and division protocols unchanged.\nClear lane eligibility in arithmetic bubbles and preserve older-store\nand completed-result ownership.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'''Assemble the exact low-half product from registered partial products\nand select it for both MEM forwarding and MEM/WB architectural\nwriteback. Preserve all load extraction, misalignment, store, hold\nand RVFI operand behavior.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'''Generalize the existing unavailable-EX-result interlock to cover\nlow-half MUL as well as loads. Keep raw source-tag matching, nonzero\nrd, consumer availability, fetch retention, one control bubble and\nbackend-hold priorities unchanged.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Wire low-half-MUL result-unavailable eligibility into the interlock\nand integrate any new lane wiring internally. Retain all top-level\nimem/dmem and nret=1 RVFI ports and retirement-order logic.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_low_mul.py','description':'''Check the actual streaming lane against exact 32-bit multiplication:\nzero, one, signed extrema interpreted modulo 2\"'^32, unsigned maxima,\nlow/high-half carry boundaries and deterministic random vectors.\nCheck consecutive independent captures, held EX/MEM payloads and\nreset. Retain every existing multiplier arithmetic vector.'\"'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain all architectural and ten-cycle progress assertions. Cover\nlow MUL to both source fields of ALU, branch, JALR, load/store and\nDIV/high-MUL consumers; consecutive dependent and independent MULs;\nx0; EX/MEM/WB forwarding; older stalled memory; arithmetic transitions;\nimem starvation; wrong-path MULs; and reset. Check one dependency\nbubble on ready buses, exact RVFI operands/data/order, stable held\nrequests and exactly-once launch, memory effects and retirement.\nInclude new RTL in explicit source lists without removing tests.'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Final streaming-MUL hypothesis validates:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
