{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_57", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r5s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261004-002-r5s1\ntitle: \"Register fully forwarded operands at the decode-to-execute boundary\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion reaches 125.68 iterations/second at 60 MHz, with\n  4,773,913 marker-bracketed cycles. Its worst path starts at an EX/MEM\n  destination tag and traverses forwarding, operand selection and the\n  ordinary ALU. The structural attempts already isolated division and\n  multiplication; another arithmetic-unit redesign would miss this\n  remaining path. The earlier registered-forwarding-selector experiment\n  improved its parent but left a WB-result mux before the execute datapath.\n  Resolving complete operand values before their capture should remove\n  both tag selection and source muxes from the beginning of fast execute.\n  LESSONS.md is absent; the experiment log was reviewed for these attempts.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move the forwarding boundary from the beginning of EX to the input of\n  the existing ID/EX operand registers. Keep the pipeline depth and the\n  blocking registered multiply/divide units. Every instruction admitted\n  to ID/EX must capture the architectural rs1 and rs2 values it will use,\n  after bypassing all older producers. EX then consumes these registered\n  values directly, without current-stage tag comparisons or a late\n  EX/MEM-versus-MEM/WB operand mux. This is a structural relocation of\n  operand acquisition, not merely registering forwarding selectors.\n\n  For each decoded source, use newest-producer priority: the current\n  advancing EX instruction's actual result, the current advancing MEM\n  instruction's final writeback value, WB, then the register file.\n  The EX candidate is the exact result about to enter EX/MEM: ordinary\n  ALU output, PC+4 for JAL/JALR, or a completed registered multiply/divide\n  result together with its saved destination and control metadata.\n  Exclude loads from this EX bypass: their address is not their result.\n  Retain the existing one-cycle load-use interlock and all bus waits.\n  The MEM candidate includes correctly extended load \n…[3661 of 6733 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2d5d9c7e7c499148]…\n_start and younger\n      decode, preserve branch/store/RVFI data and arithmetic ownership.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Expose qualified final MEM writeback data and producer metadata,\n      including extended loads and misalignment write suppression, while\n      preserving the existing external request and retirement semantics.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire the new decode bypass candidates, ready qualification and\n      captured operand interface without changing external IO or nret.\n      Preserve the existing load-use and bus/arithmetic stall controls.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain existing checks and cover both source dependencies at EX,\n      MEM and WB distances, two writers to one register, load-use stalls,\n      JAL links, completed M results, early divides, x0, trap suppression\n      and consumers held after older producers drain. Check exact RVFI\n      operands/results/order and exactly-once stores under both bus stalls.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
