{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_30", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-003-r11s2.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261004-003-r11s2\ntitle: \\\"Validated carry prediction cuts the ADD/SUB forwarding recurrence\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 204.02 iter/sec at 91.231 MHz over 4,471,643\n  bracketed cycles. Its reported worst path goes from ID/EX alu_b through\n  the EX ALU, EX forwarding, and decode operand capture back to ID/EX\n  alu_b. The accepted r10s2 predictor already removed most direct-control\n  bubbles and isolated recovery from that operand path; r9s2 showed why\n  adding late recovery to payload capture can lose the clock-rate benefit.\n  LESSONS.md is absent. The prior predictor log contains direction/target\n  prediction attempts but no arithmetic carry prediction. The promising\n  remaining predictor angle is to shorten the actual forwarding recurrence,\n  while validating each predicted carry before any dependent instruction\n  becomes valid.\n\n  A read-only run of the canonical 2K, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 ELF through the\n  unchanged reference ISS counted 2,922,016 instructions between markers,\n  915,184 eligible ADD/ADDI/SUB/AUIPC producers, and 236,980 immediate raw\n  source-tag dependencies on those producers, with no traps or OOB access.\n  A 16-entry carry-agree model predicting the carry into bit 12 produced\n  1,371 wrong forwarded carries among those dependencies. Each wrong\n  prediction needs one bubble, approximately 0.031 percent of the current\n  bracketed cycle count. This is an immediate-training retired-trace model,\n  not stalled RTL simulation or a timing measurement. Its small cycle cost\n  gives a clock-rate improvement a chance to clear the 4.6 percent gate.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one arithmetic carry predictor solely for ordinary ADD/SUB forwarding.\n  Keep the architectural ALU, memory-address generation, branch predictor,\n  registered multiplication/division, and producer retirement exact. Change\n  only the EX result supplied to younger decode operands for valid fast\n  register-writing ALU_ADD/ALU_SUB instructions, excluding jumps and loads.\n  This includes ADD, ADDI, SUB and AUIPC with their already captured alu_a\n  and alu_b. Other fast operations, JAL/JALR link values and registered\n  M-extension completions keep their existing exact forwarding candidates.\n\n  Normalize arithmetic as A + X + cin modulo 2\"'^32, with X=B, cin=0 for\n  ADD and X=~B, cin=1 for SUB. Maintain 16 two-bit agree counters indexed\n  by PC[5:2] XOR PC[9:6], initialized to weak agreement (10). Lookup reads\n  stored state using the fetch PC only; capture the agreement MSB with the\n  instruction in ID/EX, independently of payload-valid/control kills.\n  In EX use bias=X[11] and guess_c12 = bias XOR !captured_agreement.\n  Train agreement against the actual carry from A[11:0]+X[11:0]+cin,\n  only when A[15:12] XOR X[15:12] is 4'\"'b1111, since only this propagate\n  case lets c12 affect the carry into bit 16. Enqueue one event per accepted\n  eligible producer, then update the current table counter on the following\n  edge. No live outcome or pending-update bypass may feed fetch lookup.\n\n  Construct a speculative forwarding result independently of the full\n  32-bit architectural ADD/SUB result. Compute the exact 17-bit low-half\n  sum low_sum={0,A[15:0]}+{0,X[15:0]}+cin. In parallel compute the five-bit\n  middle sum mid_sum={0,A[15:12]}+{0,X[15:12]}. Let mid_prop be the reduction\n  AND of A[15:12] XOR X[15:12], and predict c16 as\n  mid_sum[4] OR (mid_prop AND guess_c12). Compute the speculative upper\n  half as A[31:16]+X[31:16]+predicted_c16, retaining its low 16 bits, and\n  concatenate it with low_sum[15:0]. The predicted high-half datapath has\n  only a four-bit carry calculation followed by a 16-bit addition, rather\n  than waiting for the producer's full 32-bit carry chain. Its lower half\n  is always exact. Do not insert the authoritative 32-bit ALU result as an\n  ADD/SUB fallback into this speculative forwarding mux: that would retain\n  the original timed recurrence. Actual producer writeback still uses the\n  unchanged exact ALU result.\n\n  Validate predicted_c16 against low_sum[16] in the same cycle, before\n  admitting a younger dependent instruction to ID/EX. A carry match proves\n  that the entire forwarded 32-bit value is exact. If the carry differs and\n  either raw younger source tag matches the nonzero EX destination with EX\n  forwarding priority, insert one interlock: let the exact producer advance\n  into EX/MEM, hold the fetch PC, and kill the younger ID/EX valid/control/\n  dispatch fields. On the next cycle the younger instruction retries at\n  the same PC using the producer's exact MEM forwarding result. Do not\n  replay or retire the producer again. If no younger raw source matches,\n  a wrong predicted carry requires no bubble. Both raw source fields must\n  participate, including instruction fields unused by execution but still\n  reported by this core's RVFI contract; otherwise an apparently harmless\n  prediction error could corrupt rs1_rdata/rs2_rdata at retirement.\n\n  The new carry interlock must use raw imemReady and source tags, the valid\n  arithmetic producer and the carry comparison, independently of its own\n  flush-qualified fetch validity. Preserve every existing imem, load-use,\n  dmem and arithmetic hold. Suppress carry-bubble acceptance behind a\n  backend hold, and train only when the producer actually advances.\n  Preserve redirect priority; a normal eligible arithmetic producer cannot\n  also be a correcting branch or jump. As in the champion's recovery\n  integration, keep the new failure signal off raw decode payload muxes\n  and payload capture enables. It holds only the fetch PC and clears ID/EX\n  validity, ctrl, fast_write, mul_class and div_class. Payload may capture\n  the unaccepted speculative values but cannot forward, launch arithmetic,\n  issue a memory request or retire. Keep the existing load-use flush-over-\n  hold behavior. A failed prediction always falls back to exact MEM data\n  after one ready-bus bubble, so even adversarial predictor state cannot\n  cause repeated replay or compromise formal forward progress.\n\n  Keep direct branch recovery_pc and raw decode visibility intact. Preserve\n  nret=1, all external ports, exact retirement order, default-illegal decoder\n  discipline, traps and full-width addresses, real RV32M and ALTOPS behavior,\n  memory bounds, and every unchanged formal/cosim/CoreMark validation gate.\n  This is a single carry-prediction hypothesis; add no load-value predictor,\n  cache, fetch queue, branch-table redesign or arithmetic latency change.\n\n  Project nine percent higher fitness with medium confidence, primarily\n  from a shorter Gowin critical forwarding path. The trace model predicts\n  negligible added cycles, but actual stalls and queued training may change\n  its count. With 1,371 added cycles, acceptance requires median Fmax above\n  approximately 95.456 MHz; a ten-percent clock improvement would yield\n  about 224.36 iter/sec. Neither is a measurement of the proposed RTL.\n  The main risks are the new carry-validation control path becoming critical,\n  synthesis reconnecting the full exact adder to speculative forwarding,\n  and the unchanged exact memory-address path limiting further Fmax. Check\n  the vendor timing report and measured marker-bracketed cycles together.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 9\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/carry_predictor.sv\n    description: |\n      Add 16 PC-folded two-bit carry-agree counters, weak-agreement reset,\n      stored-state asynchronous lookup and registered exactly-once accepted\n      updates. Train current counters on ambiguous upper-nibble propagation;\n      allow queued consume/enqueue without live resolution bypass.\n  - file: rtl/carry_forward.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the exact low-half sum, biased c12 prediction, four-bit middle\n      carry calculation, speculative high-half adder and c16 validation.\n      Expose speculative data, mismatch, ambiguous-propagate and actual\n      carry agreement. Preserve all bits modulo 2\"'^32 for ADD and SUB.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the captured carry-agreement MSB to IF/ID and ID/EX metadata.\n      Preserve existing branch metadata and downstream architectural bundles.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture carry lookup state with the instruction while preserving\n      independent raw payload capture and valid/control/dispatch kills.\n      Carry failure must not mux operands or alter payload capture enables.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Instantiate speculative carry forwarding for valid fast ADD/SUB\n      producers, leaving their authoritative EX/MEM ALU result exact.\n      Export qualified carry mismatch and queued training events. Keep\n      all other forwarding, traps, redirects and arithmetic handshakes intact.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Add a carry-failure source-tag interlock that holds only fetch and\n      inserts an ID/EX control bubble, while the exact producer advances.\n      Check both raw source tags and nonzero EX destination independently\n      of flush-qualified validity; preserve all existing stalls and priority.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire the carry predictor'\"'s fetch lookup/captured state, EX validation\n      and training, and hazard insertion with no external IO or RVFI changes.\n      Keep the full exact ALU result out of ADD/SUB decode forwarding.\n  - file: test/test_carry_predictor.py\n    description: |\n      Check folded collisions, both biases, saturation, ambiguous-only\n      training, old-state lookup, back-to-back queued events, holds and reset.\n      Exercise opposite carry patterns so correctness never relies on learning.\n  - file: test/test_carry_forward.py\n    description: |\n      Compare exact/predicted carry and 32-bit forwarded value against integer\n      ADD/SUB across byte/nibble/halfword boundaries, +/-1, signed extrema,\n      full-word wrap, every predicted-state value and deterministic random\n      operands. Prove a matching c16 implies exact data and mismatches alter\n      only the upper half; cover both carry-error directions.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all existing assertions. Exercise predicted ADD/ADDI/SUB/AUIPC\n      chains, both matching sources, x0 and raw unused source fields, exact\n      match/no-bubble and wrong-carry/one-bubble paths. Include dependent\n      loads/stores/branches/JALR/M-extension instructions, imem/dmem holds,\n      reset and back-to-back predictor events. Assert exact RVFI operands,\n      PC/order/traps, one producer retirement, no wrong memory request or\n      arithmetic launch, exact MEM fallback and bounded ready-bus progress.\n''')\nprint('Replaced hypothesis with validated carry prediction:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
