{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_40", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-003-r14s2.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261004-003-r14s2\ntitle: \\\"Validate carry-predicted forwarding in MEM and repair held EX operands\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 220.50 iterations/sec at 92.886 MHz over\n  4,212,505 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md is absent.\n  Predictor history includes successful preselected branch recovery,\n  two unsuccessful arithmetic-carry predictors, and r13s2 training\n  eligibility separation, which preserved cycles but lost 5.99 percent\n  fitness. In r12s2, byte carry prediction added only 4,263 cycles to\n  its parent, but the critical path ran through carry validation to the\n  fetch PC enable and median Fmax fell to 86.633 MHz. This points to\n  the validation boundary, rather than prediction accuracy, as the\n  obstacle. The exact ADD/SUB EX-to-ID forwarding recurrence still\n  limits timing once branch-control paths are shortened.\n\n  A scratch branch-comparator prototype in this research turn passed\n  CoreMark CRCs at unchanged cycles but reached only 90.064 MHz median\n  Gowin Fmax, so it is not the submitted design. A read-only architectural\n  trace also found only 13,671 correct last-value guesses among 204,039\n  dependent loads for a tagged 64-entry predictor. The final hypothesis\n  is solely arithmetic carry prediction with registered validation and\n  in-place operand repair.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use byte-parallel carry-agree prediction only for ADD, ADDI, SUB and\n  AUIPC forwarding, but defer validation to the existing EX/MEM register.\n  Keep the exact architectural ALU result on its original EX/MEM path.\n  The predicted result may supply younger ID operands; it must never\n  become architectural writeback, a memory address, a JALR target or\n  an unvalidated committed branch outcome. No pipeline stage, retirement\n  width change, arithmetic shortcut or load-value speculation is added.\n\n  Add three 16-entry two-bit carry-agree banks indexed by producer\n  PC[5:2] XOR PC[9:6], reset to weak agreement. Lookup uses stored state\n  only; capture their three MSBs with the producer before ID/EX, holding\n  them with its instruction. For effective addition operands X and Y,\n  let X = alu_a and Y = alu_b for addition/AUIPC, or Y = ~alu_b for SUB,\n  with initial carry zero or one respectively. At byte boundaries 8,\n  16 and 24, use X's bit at that boundary as the static carry bias,\n  XOR inverted counter MSB to obtain the guessed incoming carry.\n  Compute four independent nine-bit byte sums in parallel; upper bytes\n  use their own guessed carries. Concatenate their low eight bits for\n  the forwarding candidate. Architecturally compute the full exact\n  ALU result independently. Structurally separate the forwarding mux\n  from exact ADD/SUB: retain exact existing forwarding for other ops\n  and completed MUL/DIV, and do not leave an unused full-width ADD/SUB\n  mux arm attached to the decode forwarding data path.\n\n  Capture with an advancing eligible EX producer into EX/MEM: its\n  predicted forwarding value, carry-prediction eligibility, and three\n  local outgoing carry bits plus validation masks. c8 is authoritative\n  unconditionally; c16 is authoritative only when the c8 guess was\n  correct; c24 only when both lower guesses were correct. These masks\n  reproduce r12s2 exact training semantics without requiring a second\n  full-width carry calculation. Do not use any live carry-check result\n  to hold fetch, clear decode, qualify an arithmetic start, select raw\n  operands, or enable a payload register in the producer's EX cycle.\n  The exact EX ALU result and predicted value cross a register before\n  any replay decision affects the frontend.\n\n  In MEM compare the registered predicted value with that producer's\n  registered exact alu_result. If they differ and the current valid\n  ID/EX consumer's raw rs1 or rs2 tag matches the producer's nonzero\n  written destination, repair that consumer in place. On this edge,\n  allow the older producer to enter MEM/WB exactly once, inhibit all\n  effects and advancement of the speculative consumer, inject a real\n  EX/MEM control bubble, hold the fetch consumer and ordinary ID/EX\n  payload, and overwrite each matching captured rs1_val/rs2_val with\n  the producer's exact registered result. Also patch alu_a when its\n  matching source is rs1 rather than AUIPC's PC, and patch alu_b when\n  its matching source is rs2 rather than an immediate. Correct the\n  architectural raw source values even when an encoding does not use\n  them as ALU inputs, preserving the existing RVFI operand convention.\n  Patch both sources when both match. Keep every other payload field,\n  predicted branch choice, recovery address and instruction PC intact.\n\n  This is a one-cycle in-place replay, not a fetch redirect or refetch.\n  The repaired instruction executes normally on the following edge;\n  the older producer is then gone from EX/MEM, so repair cannot repeat.\n  An incorrect guess with no dependent current EX consumer causes no\n  replay. Correct guesses retain ordinary one-cycle dependent issue.\n  MEM forwarding and architectural writeback always use exact results,\n  so later consumers cannot inherit an incorrect prediction. During\n  repair, suppress consumer forward eligibility, branch redirect,\n  branch/carry training, MUL/DIV launch and result acceptance, and all\n  consumer memory/retirement eligibility. A speculative branch must\n  never redirect before repair. No speculative arithmetic operation\n  may start and then require cancellation. The held consumer has not\n  launched a blocking unit because its older producer was a normal\n  fast ALU operation; gate launches directly with repair.\n\n  Keep backend hold priority authoritative. Define repair only for an\n  advancing valid nonmemory/nonblocking fast producer in MEM, a valid\n  dependent EX consumer, and no existing backend hold. Such a producer\n  has no dmem request, so raw dmemReady=0 alone must not block repair.\n  Override a load-use bubble caused by a speculative EX load: suppress\n  that consumer's normal EX effects and retain its validity for repair,\n  rather than clearing the held consumer through flush-over-hold.\n  On the retry, ordinary load-use behavior resumes. A branch correction\n  from the speculative consumer is suppressed while repair is active;\n  after correction of its operands it resolves normally. Preserve\n  independent skid producer acceptance while the fetch consumer holds.\n  Reset clears prediction/repair eligibility and queued events.\n\n  Train once from accepted eligible MEM producers using saved outgoing\n  carry bits and authoritative masks, regardless of whether a dependent\n  consumer needed repair. Form agreement with the producer's saved\n  boundary biases. Queue an event and on the following edge saturate\n  the CURRENT indexed counters; consume during subsequent holds and\n  permit simultaneous consume/enqueue. Never train from guessed lower\n  carries, stale counter snapshots or a repaired consumer before its\n  real EX advancement. Keep the existing branch agree predictor,\n  old-state lookup, training delay and recovery unchanged.\n\n  The expected gain is eight percent with low confidence: byte sums\n  shorten speculative EX-to-ID data while registered comparison removes\n  the same-cycle carry-validation-to-fetch path that defeated r12s2.\n  Its 4,263 replay cycles were only about 0.095 percent of that run;\n  this is historical evidence, not a measured replay count for this\n  champion or the new validation latency. At the champion cycle count,\n  the 4.6 percent gate needs fitness above 230.643 iterations/sec and\n  approximately 97.159 MHz median Fmax. With roughly 0.1 percent extra\n  cycles, an eight-percent gain needs about 100.42 MHz. These are\n  projections. The unchanged divider, table routing and new repair\n  control fanout may still limit timing. Validate the replay protocol\n  and measure actual stalled CoreMark cycles and median Gowin Fmax.\n\n  Preserve nret=1, every external IO/RVFI port, strict retirement order,\n  exact source values and architectural ALU/memory/control results,\n  default-illegal decode, all alignment traps and full-width targets,\n  memory bounds, real RV32M and ALTOPS results/handshakes, correction\n  priority and every existing evaluation gate. In-place single-cycle\n  repair must retain the existing ten-cycle ready-bus retirement-gap\n  bound, including a mispredicted producer immediately followed by\n  DIV/REM; do not weaken liveness or remove progress assertions.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/carry_predictor.sv\n    description: |\n      Add three compact carry-agree banks with stored-state lookup,\n      queued masked MEM training, current-counter saturation, concurrent\n      consume/enqueue and deterministic reset. Never bypass live events.\n  - file: rtl/carry_forward.sv\n    description: |\n      Add independent nine-bit byte sums, biased guessed carries and\n      the forwarding candidate. Export local outgoing carry outcomes\n      and authoritative lower-check masks for registered MEM training;\n      no live validation signal may drive replay or frontend enables.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Extend ID/EX with saved carry predictions and EX/MEM with predicted\n      value, eligible producer bit, index, biases, outcomes and masks.\n      Preserve exact architectural payload and all RVFI-related fields.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture carry predictions with each admitted producer. Add explicit\n      priority for in-place EX consumer repair, patching matching raw\n      source values and selected ALU inputs while holding all other\n      instruction metadata and preserving consumer validity/controls.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use the carry candidate exclusively for eligible ADD/SUB forwarding;\n      keep exact architectural result capture and all other forwarding.\n      Register validation metadata, suppress every speculative consumer\n      effect during repair and emit a real EX/MEM bubble as older work\n      drains. Keep current branch recovery and MUL/DIV arithmetic intact.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Compare registered predicted/exact producer results, expose exact\n      repair value and qualified producer metadata, and enqueue exactly\n      one masked training event per advancing eligible producer. Keep\n      MEM forwarding, architectural writeback and memory outputs exact.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the one-cycle repair hold for fetch and ID/EX without holding\n      EX/MEM or MEM/WB. Give repair priority over speculative load-use\n      flushes while preserving backend holds, retry interlocks and\n      corrected branch recovery. Avoid validity/control loops.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire stored carry lookup, registered producer validation, current\n      EX dependency matching, exact operand repair and masked training.\n      Keep top-level ports, retirement order and nret=1 unchanged.\n  - file: test/test_carry_predictor.py\n    description: |\n      Check bias, folding, saturation, saved masks, old-state lookup,\n      exactly-once queued consumption, simultaneous events and reset\n      against an independent predictor model; retain branch-table tests.\n  - file: test/test_carry_forward.py\n    description: |\n      Check all carry guesses, ADD/ADDI/SUB/AUIPC arithmetic, byte\n      boundaries, extrema and deterministic random operands. Assert\n      each enabled training outcome is authoritative and correct\n      guesses reconstruct the exact 32-bit result.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all architectural, arithmetic and ten-cycle progress checks.\n      Update bundle observers and source lists; force correct and wrong\n      carry guesses for dependent consumers using either/both raw source\n      tags, unused tags and x0. Cover immediate and AUIPC input repair,\n      branch/JALR, load/store, consecutive producers, MUL/DIV/REM, skid\n      holds, bus stalls, reset and masked training. Assert one repair\n      cycle, held consumer metadata, exact RVFI operands/PC/data/order,\n      no speculative launch/redirect/store, exactly-once effects, and\n      the ten-cycle gap for a repaired consumer that is DIV/REM.\n''')\nprint('Revised hypothesis written:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
