{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_19", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,130p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-003-r1s2.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261004-003-r1s2\ntitle: \"Fetch-stage bimodal prediction with direct branch targets\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  This core has no registered IF/ID stage: the instruction at the fetch PC\n  is decoded directly into ID/EX. Nevertheless, every taken conditional\n  branch and every JAL/JALR currently redirects in EX and discards the\n  younger fetch, even when the destination is predictable. CoreMark's\n  repeated loops and direct calls provide opportunities to eliminate\n  these bubbles under the same approximately 22% instruction/data stalls.\n  The only recorded experiment is the baseline; there are no previous\n  predictor attempts or LESSONS.md entries. Its 199.837 ns worst path runs\n  through forwarding and the combinational M-extension ALU, so a small\n  fetch predictor has plausible timing headroom, although routing must\n  still be measured with Gowin.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one lightweight fetch-stage bimodal predictor: 64 PC-indexed\n  two-bit saturating direction counters, indexed by PC[7:2], reset to\n  weakly not taken (01). Use the counter MSB only for positively validated\n  conditional BRANCH encodings (funct3 0, 1, 4, 5, 6, 7). Generate the\n  predicted target directly as fetch PC plus the sign-extended B immediate.\n  Predict legal direct JAL unconditionally using its J immediate. Keep\n  JALR and all other instructions sequential. Do not add a BTB, global\n  history, return stack, instruction cache, or extra pipeline stage.\n  This costs 128 direction bits plus prediction metadata and a direct\n  target adder, without cached targets or tag comparators. Direction-table\n  aliasing can affect accuracy but cannot supply a stale target.\n\n  Carry the exact predicted next PC through the combinational IF/ID bundle\n  and the