{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_22", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,245p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-003-r2s2.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261004-003-r2s2\ntitle: \"Tagged cached-target prediction on the multicycle-divider champion\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion scores 82.05 iter/sec at 36.852 MHz and has no\n  branch prediction. Every taken branch, JAL, and JALR discards a younger\n  fetch even though CoreMark repeatedly executes the same control flow.\n  The prior predictor experiment reduced marker-bracketed cycles from\n  4,491,485 to 4,201,766, but synthesized the old combinational divider\n  and reached only 5.370 MHz. That result does not measure prediction on\n  the current champion. There is no LESSONS.md in this fixture.\n\n  The champion's reported critical path crosses EX, IF, the register\n  file, and early divider launch. Computing B/J targets from fetched\n  instruction bits and adding a correction-controlled instruction mux\n  is therefore unattractive. Cached targets can remove recurring control\n  bubbles without a fetch-data-to-target-adder path. The acceptance\n  threshold is 85.83 iter/sec, so preserving Fmax is essential.\n\n  A read-only reference run of the existing canonical CoreMark ELF\n  counted 2,922,015 instructions between the markers, including 529,588\n  conditional branches, 49,367 JALs and 13,735 JALRs, without traps or\n  out-of-bounds accesses. A sequential 64-entry tagged BTB model using\n  index PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8] made 58,584 next-PC errors, versus 357,527\n  existing taken-control redirects: 298,943 potential corrections saved.\n  Assuming one cycle saved per correction against the champion's logged\n  4,491,485 cycles gives 7.13 percent throughput improvement at fixed\n  Fmax. A 128-entry folded-index model gives only 7.43 percent, so choose\n  64 entries to reduce lookup and routing cost. This is a retired-trace\n  estimate with immediate training, not RTL/stall simulation or timing.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one small tagged next-PC predictor to the existing single-retire\n  pipeline: a 64-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer with a two-bit\n  saturating conditional direction counter per entry. Inde\n…[7397 of 10469 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ca743388e2ee030c]…\nource matching with fetched instruction validity rather\n      than requiring flushed instruction bits to become a NOP. Preserve\n      imem bubbles, dmem holds, divider draining and recovery priority.\n  - file: test/test_branch_predictor.py\n    description: |\n      Check cold/reset behavior, both saturation limits, replacement and\n      full-tag mismatch, conditional direction reversals, unconditional\n      and changing JALR targets, invalidation, safe target admission,\n      delayed update qualification, and same-index lookup/update behavior.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain existing checks and add warm-loop bubble reduction, JAL and\n      JALR prediction, aliased BTB entries, stale predictions on normal,\n      illegal and DIV/REM instructions, misaligned control traps, and\n      recovery coincident with imem/dmem and load-use holds. Check exact\n      retirement PC/order/operands, no wrong-path stores or divider\n      launches, and the existing ready-bus divider liveness bound.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
