{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r.get('category') == 'predictor':\n  print(json.dumps({k:r.get(k) for k in ['id','title','fitness','delta_pct','cycles','fmax_mhz','lut4','ff','outcome','critical_path','error','implementation_notes']}, indent=2))\n  h=r.get('hypothesis',{})\n  if isinstance(h,dict): print(h.get('hypothesis',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20261004-003-r1s2\",\n  \"title\": \"Fetch-stage bimodal prediction with direct branch targets\",\n  \"fitness\": 12.78,\n  \"delta_pct\": 5.45,\n  \"cycles\": 4201766,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 5.37,\n  \"lut4\": 12490,\n  \"ff\": 621,\n  \"outcome\": \"regression\",\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_2_s0\",\n    \"to\": \"dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -187.322,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 197.617,\n    \"logic_cells\": 153,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_fwd\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"error\": null,\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\"\n}\nAdd one lightweight fetch-stage bimodal predictor: 64 PC-indexed\ntwo-bit saturating direction counters, indexed by PC[7:2], reset to\nweakly not taken (01). Use the counter MSB only for positively validated\nconditional BRANCH encodings (funct3 0, 1, 4, 5, 6, 7). Generate the\npredicted target directly as fetch PC plus the sign-extended B immediate.\nPredict legal direct JAL unconditionally using its J immediate. Keep\nJALR and all other instructions sequential. Do not add a BTB, global\nhistory, return stack, instruction cache, or extra pipeline stage.\nThis costs 128 direction bits plus prediction metadata and a direct\ntarget adder, without cached targets or tag comparators. Direction-table\naliasing can affect accuracy but cannot supply a stale target.\n\nCarry the exact predicted next PC through the combinational IF/ID bundle\nand the existing ID/EX register. Advance the fetch PC to that value only\nwhen the fetched instruction is actually accepted: memory is ready,\nload-use/data-memory holds are absent, and no correction flush is active.\nPreserve existing bubble behavior when imem is not ready. Predictions\nmust never flush the branch itself or invalidate its fetched successor.\n\nIn EX, derive the architectural next PC from the existing forwarded\nbranch comparison, JAL/JALR target calculation, and misalignment-trap\nrules. Redirect and flush the younger instruction only when this actual\nnext PC differs from the carried prediction. Perform this \n…[16804 of 19876 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1b5e75e015ab4f9a]…\nne. Preserve the current\ndivider arithmetic, launch latency, result protocol and liveness bound.\n\nSubtracting the trace model's 286,698 saved one-cycle corrections from\n4,491,485 cycles projects 4,204,787 cycles, a 6.82 percent throughput\ngain at constant Fmax. The earlier asynchronous predictor's RTL run\nwas about 2,190 cycles above its corresponding trace projection;\napplying that small discrepancy provisionally gives about 4.207\nmillion cycles and 115.1 iter/sec at 48.433 MHz. Exceeding 107.83 by\n4.6 percent requires about 112.79 iter/sec and roughly 47.45 MHz,\nallowing only about two percent clock loss. These are estimates;\nstall phase, stale direction during holds and placed routing require\nmeasurement. The experiment tests whether registered small-table\nlookup can preserve this margin. Expect roughly seven percent higher\nfitness, with medium confidence. Preserve\nnret=1, the entire IO/RVFI contract, decoder legality, arithmetic and\nevery unchanged formal, cosim, CoreMark CRC/marker/UART and Gowin gate.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
