n-table\naliasing can affect accuracy but cannot supply a stale target.\n\nCarry the exact predicted next PC through the combinational IF/ID bundle\nand the existing ID/EX register. Advance the fetch PC to that value only\nwhen the fetched instruction is actually accepted: memory is ready,\nload-use/data-memory holds are absent, and no correction flush is active.\nPreserve existing bubble behavior when imem is not ready. Predictions\nmust never flush the branch itself or invalidate its fetched successor.\n\nIn EX, derive the architectural next PC from the existing forwarded\nbranch comparison, JAL/JALR target calculation, and misalignment-trap\nrules. Redirect and flush the younger instruction only when this actual\nnext PC differs from the carried\n…[28877 of 31949 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0568321aa000d54b]…\ns EX, so\nthe mandatory empty-EX guard prevents a wrong-path early divide.\nChoose raw decode versus EX operands only by registered EX occupancy.\nPreserve divider latency, result acceptance, ready-bus liveness bounds\nand the champion's captured alu_a/alu_b and rs1_val/rs2_val paths.\n\nThe trace model saves 302,604 one-cycle corrections, projecting\n4,471,309 cycles and about 141.61 iter/sec at 63.318 MHz, a 6.8 percent\ngain. More than 4.6 percent above 132.63 requires over 138.73098\niter/sec, or about 62.03 MHz at that projected cycle count. Delayed\ntraining, bus-stall phase and placed routing require evaluation; there\nis only about two percent clock margin. Preserve nret=1, every IO/RVFI\nfield, decoder legality, arithmetic and all formal, cosim, CRC, bounds,\nMMIO-marker, UART and Gowin evaluation checks unchanged.\n\nNOTES: \nPATH: {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_div/divisor_q_4_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_div/result_21_s0', 'slack_ns': -12.214, 'arrival_ns': 22.508, 'logic_cells': 69, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_div']}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
