{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor' and d.get('round_id') in [4,5]:\n  print(d['id'],d['title'])\n  print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\n  print(d.get('critical_path'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-003-r4s2 Compact registered lookahead prediction without recovery feedback\nAdd one 32-entry two-bit bimodal branch-direction predictor with a\nregistered lookahead lookup, plus unconditional direct-JAL prediction.\nKeep the current combinational fetch/decode and all pipeline stages.\nIndex counters by PC[6:2], reset them to weakly not taken, and update\nthe indexed current counter once per advancing valid legal conditional\nbranch. Use explicit clocked counter bits, asynchronous lookup into\nthe lookahead register, and no combinational EX-update bypass.\n\nThe fetch PC and its direction prediction must be one transaction.\nFor the current raw fetched instruction, positively recognize legal\nBRANCH funct3 values and JAL, form the exact B/J immediate, and compute\none shared PC-relative target. Select that target for a branch only\nwhen the registered direction is valid and taken; select it for JAL\nunconditionally. Otherwise select PC+4. Predict transfers only when\nsource and target are word aligned and inside instruction RAM\n[0x00000000, 0x00100000). Leave JALR sequential. There is no BTB,\nstored target, tag comparison, global history or return stack.\n\nOn an ordinary accepted fetch edge, update the PC to this selected\nnormal next PC and simultaneously capture the counter MSB indexed\nby that same normal next PC into a direction register, marking it\nvalid for the new PC. Prepare two small parallel old-state MSB reads:\none at (PC[6:2]+1) modulo 32, and one at\n(PC[6:2]+B_or_J_immediate[6:2]) modulo 32. Select between their\noutputs with the same admitted predicted_transfer used to choose\nthe next PC. For admitted aligned transfers these modular indices\nexactly match next_PC[6:2]. Do not feed the full-width target adder,\nits range check and next-PC mux serially into an indexed table read;\ncompute both five-bit indices and table reads independently, and\napply the admission/transfer choice only at the final one-bit mux.\nThis keeps full-width target validation from preceding a counter\nread, while adding only a second smal\n…[8053 of 11125 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c189e861129efc5b]…\nrather than\ninstruction events, and change stall phase; this projection is not a\nmeasured result. Clearing 4.6 percent above 125.68 requires over\n131.46128 iter/sec, implying about 58.97 MHz at that projected cycle\ncount: there is little clock margin. A standalone arithmetic check\nexhausted all 524,288 aligned JAL word displacements at seven source\nPCs spanning RAM boundaries and the midpoint, 3,670,016 cases total;\nthe high-bit admission rule and reconstructed targets matched exact\nmathematical range/alignment checks in every case. Keep these boundary\nproperties covered in implementation tests, and rely on unchanged\nGowin timing and CoreMark measurement for acceptance. Preserve\nnret=1, all external IO/RVFI ports, decoder legality, arithmetic and\nevery formal, cosim, CRC, marker, UART and bounds check.\n\n{'from': 'cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_0_s0', 'to': 'dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -12.104, 'arrival_ns': 22.398, 'logic_cells': 30, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_fwd', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
