dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -6.719, \"arrival_ns\": 17.013, \"logic_cells\": 9, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}, \"implementation_notes\": \"\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20261004-003-r7s2\", \"title\": \"Agree prediction with registered direct recovery targets\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 169.26, \"fmax_mhz\": 75.685, \"cycles\": 4471643, \"lut4\": 4024, \"ff\": 986, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs2_val_0_s0\", \"to\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_div/quotient_negative_q_s0\", \"slack_ns\": -8.656, \"arrival_ns\": 18.95, \"logic_cells\": 40, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_predictor\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_div\"]}, \"implementation_notes\": \"\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  h=d['hypothesis']\n  print(d['id'],d['title'])\n  print(h['motivation'] if isinstance(h,dict) else d['motivation'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  h=d['hypothesis']\n  print(d['id'],d['title'])\n  print(h['motivation'] if isinstance(h,dict) else d['motivation'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-003-r1s2 Fetch-stage bimodal prediction with direct branch targets\nThis core has no registered IF/ID stage: the instruction at the fetch PC\nis decoded directly into ID/EX. Nevertheless, every taken conditional\nbranch and every JAL/JALR currently redirects in EX and discards the\nyounger fetch, even when the destination is predictable. CoreMark's\nrepeated loops and direct calls provide opportunities to eliminate\nthese bubbles under the same approximately 22% instruction/data stalls.\nThe only recorded experiment is the baseline; there are no previous\npredictor attempts or LESSONS.md entries. Its 199.837 ns worst path runs\nthrough forwarding and the combinational M-extension ALU, so a small\nfetch predictor has plausible timing headroom, although routing must\nstill be measured with Gowin.\n\nA read-only run of the existing CoreMark ELF through the unmodified\nreference ISS counted 2,922,015 instructions between its MMIO markers,\nincluding 529,588 conditional branches (294,425 taken), 49,367 JALs,\nand 13,735 JALRs, with no traps or out-of-bounds accesses. A sequential\ntwo-bit direction model with 64 entries produced 51,857 conditional\nnext-PC misses, versus 111,175 direction misses for static backward-taken\nprediction. Removing direct-JAL corrections as well gives 291,935 net\ncorrections saved. Against the recorded 4,491,485 baseline cycles,\na one-cycle-per-correction model projects 6.95% throughput improvement\nat unchanged Fmax. This is an estimate, not an RTL/stall simulation or\ntiming result. A 256-entry model only raised that estimate to 7.15%,\nso the smaller 64-entry table is the proposed implementation.\n\nhyp-20261004-003-r2s2 Tagged cached-target prediction on the multicycle-divider champion\nThe current champion scores 82.05 iter/sec at 36.852 MHz and has no\nbranch prediction. Every taken branch, JAL, and JALR discards a younger\nfetch even though CoreMark repeatedly executes the same control flow.\nThe prior predictor experiment reduced marker-bracketed cycles from\n4,491,485 to 4,201,766, but synthesized\n…[6481 of 9553 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:fa8902663f32aacd]…\nr updates delayed by two, three and\nfour architectural instruction events retained those same 41,188\nmisses. Agreement learning reduces destructive interference between\noppositely biased branches sharing an entry. These are trace estimates,\nnot RTL, random-stall or Gowin measurements.\n\nhyp-20261004-003-r7s2 Agree prediction with registered direct recovery targets\nThe current champion scores 154.12 iter/sec at 73.574 MHz and needs\n4,773,913 cycles for the canonical ten-iteration CoreMark run.\nLESSONS.md is absent. Six previous predictor experiments passed\ncorrectness but lost their tournament rounds: recurring control\nbubbles were removed, while their clock rates failed to beat the\narithmetic improvements competing in those rounds. The last agree\npredictor needed 4,471,643 cycles, but ran on the previous divider.\nThe current champion's reported path still crosses EX, IF and early\ndivider launch. Adding prediction without shortening that recovery\npath leaves only about two percent clock margin for acceptance.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"agent_message","text":"The previous agree predictor cut CoreMark cycles from 4,773,913 to 4,471,643, but its worst timing path ran through predictor recovery into early divider launch. The current champion launches division from registered EX operands, which removes that coupling. I’m focusing on a compact agree predictor that preserves this boundary."}}
