{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_17", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('id') in ['hyp-20261004-003-r3s2','hyp-20261004-003-r5s2']:\n  print(d['id'])\n  print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-003-r3s2\nAdd a 64-entry, two-bit bimodal conditional direction predictor indexed\nby fetch PC[7:2], plus unconditional prediction of legal direct JAL.\nReset the counters to weakly not taken. Use two explicit banks of\n64 clocked bits, with asynchronous indexed reads and synchronous\nupdates; do not forward the current EX update into the fetch lookup.\nRead the old indexed counter for saturating training, not a carried\nstale counter snapshot. Only advancing, valid, legal conditional\nbranches update the table, at most once each. Direction aliasing is\nallowed, but the current fetched instruction supplies the target.\nThere is no BTB, target memory, tag compare, global history or RAS.\n\nPositively predecode legal BRANCH funct3 values and direct JAL from\nraw imem_data. Form the B/J immediate and share one fetch PC-relative\ntarget adder. A branch predicts a transfer only when its counter MSB\nis one; JAL predicts a transfer unconditionally. Admit predictions\nonly for word-aligned source and target addresses in instruction RAM\n[0x00000000, 0x00100000). Otherwise predict PC+4 and let ordinary EX\nhandling preserve architectural control flow and misalignment traps.\nKeep JALR sequential and resolve it through the existing EX path.\nUpdate the PC only when fetch really advances; holds never consume\na prediction, and an accepted correction overrides imem backpressure.\n\nCarry only a predicted_transfer bit with each accepted instruction\nthrough IF/ID and ID/EX. Because prediction is qualified by the\nactual instruction and uses its exact B/J immediate, EX need not\ncompare two 32-bit next-PC buses. Derive actual_transfer from the\nexisting forwarded branch outcome or jump control, suppressed by\nthe existing target-misalignment fault. Redirect when actual_transfer\nXOR predicted_transfer is true, qualified by valid and EX advance.\nFor an actual transfer recover to the authoritative EX target; for\na predicted transfer resolving to fall-through recover to the\ninstruction's own PC+4. Correct predictions leave the successor\nvalid. \n…[6141 of 9213 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b0068989e01fc013]…\nions from\n4,773,913 champion cycles projects 4,485,679 cycles and 133.76 iter/sec\nat 60 MHz, roughly 6.4 percent higher fitness. Pipeline bubbles and\nmemory/arithmetic holds advance the update queue in clocks rather than\ninstruction events, and change stall phase; this projection is not a\nmeasured result. Clearing 4.6 percent above 125.68 requires over\n131.46128 iter/sec, implying about 58.97 MHz at that projected cycle\ncount: there is little clock margin. A standalone arithmetic check\nexhausted all 524,288 aligned JAL word displacements at seven source\nPCs spanning RAM boundaries and the midpoint, 3,670,016 cases total;\nthe high-bit admission rule and reconstructed targets matched exact\nmathematical range/alignment checks in every case. Keep these boundary\nproperties covered in implementation tests, and rely on unchanged\nGowin timing and CoreMark measurement for acceptance. Preserve\nnret=1, all external IO/RVFI ports, decoder legality, arithmetic and\nevery formal, cosim, CRC, marker, UART and bounds check.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
