{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=[json.loads(s) for s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()]\nfor r in rows:\n    if r['id'] in {'hyp-20261004-001-r8s0','hyp-20261004-001-r9s0','hyp-20261004-001-r10s0','hyp-20261004-003-r13s2','hyp-20261004-003-r14s2','hyp-20261004-002-r12s1','hyp-20261004-001-r13s0'}:\n        h=r.get('hypothesis',{})\n        print('\\\\n'+r['id']+' '+r['title'])\n        print(h.get('motivation',''))\n        if r['id'] in {'hyp-20261004-003-r13s2','hyp-20261004-001-r8s0','hyp-20261004-001-r9s0','hyp-20261004-002-r12s1'}: print(h.get('hypothesis',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nhyp-20261004-001-r8s0 Predecode EX dispatch and forwarding eligibility into three ID/EX bits\nThe champion scores 174.53 iterations/sec at median 83.321 MHz and\n4,773,913 marker-bracketed cycles. Its reported worst path starts at\nID/EX ctrl.alu_op[4] and crosses EX into the timed memory circuit.\nA fresh unchanged vendor build reproduced placement-zero Fmax\n85.334 MHz and the same 17.013 ns operation-control path. EX repeatedly\nclassifies the binary operation and combines instruction properties\nto authorize normal-result forwarding. Those controls participate in\nthe EX-result-to-decode recurrence. LESSONS.md is absent; the prior\nmicro_opt entries address divider arithmetic and forwarding selection,\nrather than predecoding this current EX instruction's dispatch and\nnormal-forwarding eligibility at its existing admission boundary.\n\nRetiming three instruction-only predicates into the existing ID/EX\nregister should shorten EX control and forwarding paths while preserving\nevery instruction's latency. Add logic fast_write, mul_class, div_class\nto id_ex_t, in that order immediately after alu_b and before imm.\nThis is a single local predecode optimization with three added control\nflip-flops; retain the current five-bit ALU opcode, ALU result mux,\narithmetic datapaths, pipeline boundaries and execution schedules.\n\nIn id_stage's decoded next-state bundle, calculate mul_class from the\nexisting decoded ALU operation equal to MUL, MULH, MULHU or MULHSU.\nCalculate div_class from operation equal to DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU.\nCompute fast_write = dec_reg_write && !dec_mem_read && !dec_is_illegal\n&& !decoded.mul_class && !decoded.div_class. These predicates describe\nthe candidate instruction before capture, independently of bus ready,\nredirect, launch and arithmetic-unit occupancy. Capture them with its\nfinal architectural operands, controls and metadata using the unchanged\nauthoritative ID/EX reset/flush/hold/invalid-bubble logic. An invalid\nbubble remains entirely zero; all three flags hold with a held instruction.\n\nIn ex_stage rep\n…[20133 of 23205 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:843eed871677c6dd]…\nstory includes successful preselected branch recovery,\ntwo unsuccessful arithmetic-carry predictors, and r13s2 training\neligibility separation, which preserved cycles but lost 5.99 percent\nfitness. In r12s2, byte carry prediction added only 4,263 cycles to\nits parent, but the critical path ran through carry validation to the\nfetch PC enable and median Fmax fell to 86.633 MHz. This points to\nthe validation boundary, rather than prediction accuracy, as the\nobstacle. The exact ADD/SUB EX-to-ID forwarding recurrence still\nlimits timing once branch-control paths are shortened.\n\nA scratch branch-comparator prototype in this research turn passed\nCoreMark CRCs at unchanged cycles but reached only 90.064 MHz median\nGowin Fmax, so it is not the submitted design. A read-only architectural\ntrace also found only 13,671 correct last-value guesses among 204,039\ndependent loads for a tagged 64-entry predictor. The final hypothesis\nis solely arithmetic carry prediction with registered validation and\nin-place operand repair.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
