{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nids=['hyp-20261004-002-r12s1','hyp-20261004-003-r13s2','hyp-20261004-002-r13s1','hyp-20261004-002-r14s1']\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in ids:\n  print(d['id'])\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print('MOTIVATION',h.get('motivation',''))\n  if d['id'].endswith('r13s2'): print('HYPOTHESIS',h.get('hypothesis',''))\n  print('TIMING',d.get('critical_path'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-002-r12s1\nMOTIVATION The champion scores 204.02 iter/sec at 91.231 MHz over 4471643\nmarker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its frontend stops requesting new\ninstructions during load-use, data-memory and blocking arithmetic waits,\nthen must face instruction-bus stalls again when decode resumes.\nLESSONS.md is absent. Relevant structural attempts are r10s1's four-entry\nunpredicted queue and r11s1's prediction-aware two-entry queue. The latter\nreduced cycles to 4135106 but its 87.761 MHz median Fmax yielded only\n212.23 iter/sec, a 4.02 percent gain below the 4.6 percent acceptance gate.\nA one-entry buffer retains most stall overlap with less storage, control\nand routing around fetch, decode and prediction recovery.\n\nA scratch prototype of this single-entry design, using the champion RTL\nelsewhere and the unchanged FPGA wrapper and stall generator, completed\nall three Gowin placement options at 86.846, 91.118 and 91.118 MHz.\nThe unchanged cosim harness ran the canonical 2K, -O3, ITERATIONS=10\nCoreMark ELF with both bus-stall flags in 4212505 bracketed cycles.\nAll five required CRCs matched, Correct operation validated. appeared,\nthe timing markers were present, and oob was false. The resulting\n216.30 iter/sec is about 6.02 percent above the champion. The prototype\nalso passed the unchanged retirement-differential harness on selftest\nand all three committed random programs, both with ready buses and with\nboth bus-stall flags: 115, 11037, 11094 and 12059 retirements per run.\nThese are prototype measurements; implementation still needs the full\nformal and standard evaluation gates. The prototype's option-zero worst\npath ended at fetch PC through EX correction, rather than the queue data.\n\nTIMING {'from': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs2_val_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_predictor/pending_valid_q_s0', 'slack_ns': -7.265, 'arrival_ns': 17.559, 'logic_cells': 37, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_predictor', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nhyp-20261004-002-r13s1\nMOTIVATION The champion delivers 220.50 iterations/sec at median 92.886 MHz over\n4,21\n…[5646 of 8718 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5c3a211f9af2fd58]…\nion scores 220.50 iterations/sec at median 92.886 MHz over\n4,212,505 bracketed CoreMark cycles. The previous structural streaming\nMUL experiment reduced cycles to 4,017,079 but duplicated product lanes,\nincreased DSP usage from two to four and lowered median Fmax to 88.485\nMHz, leaving fitness at 220.27. The current blocking multiplier captures\nraw operands even though ID/EX already holds their final values, then\nspends separate clocks registering products and a final result. Removing\nthese redundant boundaries for the common low-half operation can recover\nthe cycle benefit with one owner and the original shared multiplier.\nNo LESSONS.md exists; structural history and the latest multiplier result\nwere reviewed. Scratch branch-comparison retimings were slower and are\nexcluded from this proposal.\n\nTIMING {'from': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs2_val_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/pc_27_s1', 'slack_ns': -7.352, 'arrival_ns': 17.647, 'logic_cells': 38, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_predictor', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if']}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
