{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_30", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r15s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261005-002-r15s1\ntitle: \"Register branch correction at EX/MEM and squash the younger EX slot\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 220.50 iterations/sec at 92.886 MHz over 4,212,505\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md is absent. Structural history\n  shows that the single-entry fetch skid buffer succeeded, while deeper\n  queues and the recent faster MUL schedules failed the acceptance gate.\n  The champion and recent descendants repeatedly report a long path\n  from registered branch operands through execute and predictor control\n  to the fetch PC. Changing predictor acceptance alone in r13s2 preserved\n  cycles but reduced Fmax. A register boundary on correction itself can\n  remove this comparison-to-frontend path without changing prediction\n  policy, ordinary ALU execution or the existing fetch buffer.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retiming control-flow correction across the existing EX/MEM boundary\n  will improve clock speed enough to pay for one extra recovery cycle\n  on a mispredicted conditional branch or an unpredicted JALR. Preserve\n  single issue, nret=1, the fetch skid buffer, agree-counter algorithm,\n  direct prediction qualification, preselected recovery addresses,\n  decode operand forwarding, multiplier and divider. This is one\n  structural change to the branch-resolution boundary.\n\n  EX still computes the exact branch condition, actual transfer,\n  alignment trap and architectural pc_next from registered operands.\n  Replace the live redirect output with a saved correction-valid bit\n  and full-width correction target in the EX/MEM entry. Capture the\n  exact current actual_transfer XOR predicted_transfer decision only\n  when the EX instruction really advances, alongside its retirement\n  metadata. The target remains JALR's rs1+imm with bit zero cleared,\n  otherwise the existing preselected recovery_pc. Do not truncate or\n  recompute architectural targets. In the following cycle only these\n  registered correction fields may drive fetch redirect, flush and\n  redirect_target; there must be \n…[4426 of 7498 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1aa63f1b11096d3e]…\netic waits, flushing ID/EX and fetch without holding the\n      correcting branch in MEM. Preserve genuine data-memory holds\n      and prevent correction/wait/flush combinational loops.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire saved correction to the existing fetch redirect interface\n      and saved MEM branch events to the predictor training interface.\n      Keep nret=1, all ports and retirement-order logic unchanged.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all current architectural and progress assertions.\n      Add correct/wrong predictions and JALR recovery with wrong-path\n      ALU, branch, load/store, MUL and DIV consumers; cover imem/dmem\n      stalls, buffered wrong-path fetch, reset, misaligned targets and\n      x0/link forwarding. Assert exactly-once correction/training,\n      no wrong-path launch/store/retirement, precise RVFI PC/operands,\n      strict order and the existing ten-cycle ready-bus retirement gap\n      after correction followed by dependent DIV/REM.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
