{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_14", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nids={'hyp-20261004-003-r6s2','hyp-20261004-003-r10s2','hyp-20261004-003-r13s2'}\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('id') in ids:\n  h=d['hypothesis']; print(d['id']); print(h.get('motivation','')); print(h.get('hypothesis',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-003-r6s2\nThe champion retires the canonical CoreMark run in 4,773,913 cycles\nat 63.318 MHz, scoring 132.63 iter/sec. LESSONS.md is absent. All five\nearlier predictor attempts passed correctness but lost their rounds;\nthe last 32-entry bimodal predictor took 4,485,269 cycles at 58.887 MHz.\nPredictor recovery and routing costs consumed its throughput gain.\nThe new champion captures fully forwarded architectural operands in\nID/EX, removing the old EX operand/ALU feedback arrangement. Preserve\nthat timing boundary when integrating a small, more accurate predictor.\n\nA fresh read-only run of the canonical 2K, -O3 reference program counted\n529,588 conditional branches, 294,425 taken branches, 49,367 direct\nJALs and 13,735 JALRs between markers, without traps or out-of-bounds\naccesses. An immediate-training trace model gave 55,558 conditional\nmisses for the previous 32-entry PC-indexed bimodal organization,\nversus 41,188 for 64 two-bit agreement counters indexed by\nPC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8], using the branch displacement sign as static\ndirection bias. Replaying counter updates delayed by two, three and\nfour architectural instruction events retained those same 41,188\nmisses. Agreement learning reduces destructive interference between\noppositely biased branches sharing an entry. These are trace estimates,\nnot RTL, random-stall or Gowin measurements.\n\nAdd one compact folded-PC agree predictor: 64 two-bit saturating\ncounters indexed by fetch PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8]. Each counter predicts\nwhether a conditional branch agrees with its static backward-taken,\nforward-not-taken bias, rather than predicting absolute taken direction.\nReset counters to weakly agree (10). For a positively validated BRANCH\nencoding, define bias_taken = instruction[31] and predicted_taken =\ncounter_MSB ? bias_taken : !bias_taken. Train with agreement =\nresolved_taken == instruction[31]. Direct legal JAL is always predicted\nto its immediate target; JALR stays sequential. Use one asynchronous\ncounter-MSB lookup, explicit clocked counter bits, no live\n…[13085 of 16157 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0ef135c80cc5c230]…\narts and their exact handshakes. Reset queued\nvalidity and counters deterministically; invalid queued payload has no\neffect. Keep nret=1, all external IO/RVFI fields, strict retirement order,\ndecoder default illegality, full-width addresses and trap semantics,\nmemory bounds, RV32M/ALTOPS behavior and every unchanged evaluation gate.\n\nExpected gain is six percent with low confidence, principally through\nGowin Fmax rather than accuracy. Training has the same one-event delay\nand admits exactly the same updates, so marker-bracketed cycles should\nremain comparable. At 4,212,505 cycles, acceptance requires more than\n230.643 iterations/sec and approximately 97.159 MHz median Fmax; a\nsix-percent gain needs approximately 98.46 MHz. These are projections,\nnot timing measurements. The unchanged divider and forwarding recurrence\nmay limit the gain, and Gowin may already factor some equivalent terms.\nVerify predictor-state equivalence and architectural traces, then score\nmeasured stalled cycles times median vendor Fmax.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
