{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('id')=='hyp-20261004-003-r2s2':print(d['hypothesis']['motivation']);print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_40", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('id')=='hyp-20261004-003-r2s2':print(d['hypothesis']['motivation']);print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "The current champion scores 82.05 iter/sec at 36.852 MHz and has no\nbranch prediction. Every taken branch, JAL, and JALR discards a younger\nfetch even though CoreMark repeatedly executes the same control flow.\nThe prior predictor experiment reduced marker-bracketed cycles from\n4,491,485 to 4,201,766, but synthesized the old combinational divider\nand reached only 5.370 MHz. That result does not measure prediction on\nthe current champion. There is no LESSONS.md in this fixture.\n\nThe champion's reported critical path crosses EX, IF, the register\nfile, and early divider launch. Computing B/J targets from fetched\ninstruction bits and adding a correction-controlled instruction mux\nis therefore unattractive. Cached targets can remove recurring control\nbubbles without a fetch-data-to-target-adder path. The acceptance\nthreshold is 85.83 iter/sec, so preserving Fmax is essential.\n\nA read-only reference run of the existing canonical CoreMark ELF\ncounted 2,922,015 instructions between the markers, including 529,588\nconditional branches, 49,367 JALs and 13,735 JALRs, without traps or\nout-of-bounds accesses. A sequential 64-entry tagged BTB model using\nindex PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8] made 58,584 next-PC errors, versus 357,527\nexisting taken-control redirects: 298,943 potential corrections saved.\nAssuming one cycle saved per correction against the champion's logged\n4,491,485 cycles gives 7.13 percent throughput improvement at fixed\nFmax. A 128-entry folded-index model gives only 7.43 percent, so choose\n64 entries to reduce lookup and routing cost. This is a retired-trace\nestimate with immediate training, not RTL/stall simulation or timing.\n\nAdd one small tagged next-PC predictor to the existing single-retire\npipeline: a 64-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer with a two-bit\nsaturating conditional direction counter per entry. Index by the six\nbits PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8], compare the remaining full PC identity, and\ncache an aligned target plus a conditional/unconditional kind bit.\nLearn conditional branches, direct\nJAL, and legal JA\n…[3851 of 6923 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3222f239019eba38]…\nh operand selection, regfile data and signed magnitude arithmetic.\nInvalid decoded payload may contain raw bits but may never launch,\nredirect, write memory, or retire. This is part of predictor integration:\nprediction recovery should control state enables, not mux every source\naddress before register-file reads and magnitude generation.\n\nExpect about seven percent higher fitness from eliminating recurring\ncontrol bubbles while preserving the champion's clock rate. The main\nrisks are direct-mapped conflicts and a new tag/target-read or correction\npath lowering Gowin Fmax. Evaluate with unchanged formal, cosim,\ncanonical CoreMark CRC/marker/UART, and median Gowin timing gates.\nThe trace estimate needs Fmax above approximately 35.982 MHz to clear\nacceptance, allowing only about 2.36 percent clock loss. Acceptance\nrequires more than 4.6 percent above 82.05, not above the old 12.12\nbaseline. Add focused predictor and pipeline tests without\nchanging the decoder's legality policy or any evaluation infrastructure.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
