{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_74","type":"agent_message","text":"None of the completed prototypes clears the acceptance threshold. I’m refining the hypothesis around prediction validation: use a balanced four-bit comparison tree and precomputed prediction polarity to replace the long comparison path without adding recovery cycles. The final file will mark the expected gain as unmeasured and low confidence."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_75", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-003-r15s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-003-r15s2',\n'title':'Balanced prediction-error validation with predecoded branch polarity',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 220.50 iterations/sec at 92.886 MHz over 4,212,505 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md is absent. Predictor history shows successful preselected recovery addresses, but r13s2 training-acceptance separation lost 5.99 percent and r14s2 carry prediction lost 13.12 percent. Recent paths repeatedly start at registered branch operands and pass through EX/predictor control to fetch. The placed reports contain a long carry cascade for relational branch comparison, followed by outcome selection, prediction comparison and eligibility logic.\n\nScratch research in this turn tested delayed conditional validation, later training, RAM-backed tables and local counter updates. None cleared acceptance. Delayed validation passed selftest/random RVFI cosim and canonical stalled CoreMark CRCs but added 41,321 bracketed cycles and reached only about 85 MHz. A prototype that predecoded prediction polarity but retained native full-width comparisons preserved 4,212,505 cycles and reached 87.722 MHz. These results do not demonstrate a gain. They motivate a different validation circuit: remove the serial wide comparator as well as the late branch-polarity selection, while retaining the existing prediction policy and recovery latency.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Implement one predictor-validation architecture: predecode the expected primitive condition and validate it with a balanced exact comparison tree in EX. Retain the current 64-entry folded-PC, two-bit agree table, sign bias, old-state lookup, one-event training delay, skid buffer, target admission, complementary recovery PC, JAL/JALR behavior and pipeline stages. No accuracy change, extra recovery cycle, arithmetic speculation, cache, BTB or global history is proposed.\n\nAll six legal branch conditions share three primitives: equal, signed less-than and unsigned less-than. Instruction funct3 bit 0 complements the selected primitive. Before ID/EX capture, form expected_primitive = admitted predicted_transfer XOR instruction[12], and bias_primitive = instruction[31] XOR instruction[12]. Capture both with the selected fetched instruction, including a buffered head; hold them with that instruction and preserve normal control-only flush behavior. Use the final admitted transfer bit, not the raw direction-table output. The primitive selector comes only from registered validated branch control.\n\nReplace native full-width signed/unsigned relational expressions in the branch validator with eight independent four-bit comparisons. For each nibble compute equal E and unsigned less L. Combine adjacent lower and upper groups with E_parent = E_upper AND E_lower and L_parent = L_upper OR (E_upper AND L_lower). Use explicit balanced 8-to-4-to-2-to-1 levels, with the more significant group always taking priority. Do not use an iterative loop accumulator, a priority chain, a 32-bit subtraction, or a parallel native full-width comparator that synthesis can retain on this control path. Whole-word equality is the tree's E result. Whole-word unsigned less is its L result. For signed less, XOR bit 31 of BOTH operands with one before the nibble comparisons, or use the equivalent sign-difference selection over the unsigned result; prefer a separate parallel top-nibble signed comparison so sign selection does not add a late level. Share lower-nibble summaries between signed and unsigned trees. This remains exact for all 32-bit operands, including signed extrema, equality and differences confined to the least significant nibble.\n\nSelect the appropriate primitive from registered branch kind, then form conditional_mismatch = legal_conditional AND direct_target_aligned AND (primitive XOR expected_primitive). A misaligned direct target cannot have an admitted taken prediction, so masking its transfer is exact for both outcomes. Keep architectural branch_cond = primitive XOR registered funct3[0] for trap discipline and resolved pc_next. Jump mismatch uses an independent legal jump/alignment predicate and captured prediction; it must not pass through conditional branch comparison or outcome-dependent branch fault logic. Redirect remains qualified by valid EX advance, data-memory hold and blocking-unit completion, with its existing recovery address selected from saved metadata. Preserve precise traps, JAL/JALR link suppression and full-width targets.\n\nForm train_agree directly as primitive == bias_primitive. Separate branch-event eligibility from the outcome-dependent training filter: enqueue valid legal advancing conditional candidates, saving agreement and train_allow = target_aligned OR NOT architectural_branch_cond. The following edge updates the current counter only when the saved allow bit is true. This is the same event delay and same updates as the existing exclusion of taken misaligned branches. It prevents the newly balanced comparator from feeding pending-valid control through a second condition/trap mux. Consume once during later stalls, allow simultaneous consumption/enqueue, clear pending eligibility on reset, and never bypass a live or queued event into prediction. Preserve counter reset and saturation policy exactly.\n\nThe single hypothesis is the balanced, prediction-conditioned branch validator; the two saved polarity bits and registered training filter are its integration. The native-comparator scratch result is negative evidence, not a timing measurement of this final circuit. Project seven percent fitness gain with low confidence. At unchanged 4,212,505 cycles, acceptance requires more than 230.643 iterations/sec and approximately 97.159 MHz median Gowin Fmax; seven percent requires approximately 99.39 MHz. Balanced LUT comparison may reduce comparator routing and downstream polarity depth, but Gowin might remap it into a carry chain, and unchanged ALU forwarding/divider paths may cap Fmax. Inspect the actual vendor cone, verify identical predictions and bracketed cycles, and score all three placement options. Do not count area reduction or an option-zero timing gain as acceptance.\n\nPreserve nret=1 and all external IO/RVFI fields, exact operands/results and next PCs, strict retirement order, default-illegal decoding, alignment traps, memory bounds, real RV32M and ALTOPS handshakes, correction priority, ready-bus liveness bounds, and every formal/cosim/CoreMark CRC/marker/UART/timing gate. No evaluation infrastructure changes.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':7,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/branch_validate.sv','description':'Add an exact branch primitive evaluator built from eight four-bit comparisons and explicit balanced significance-preserving E/L merges. Share lower summaries for signed/unsigned comparison and keep the control path free of a native 32-bit relational or subtract expression.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Add expected-primitive and static-bias-primitive bits to ID/EX without changing architectural payload or retirement width.'},\n {'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'Precompute both primitive polarities from instruction funct3/sign and the admitted prediction of the selected raw or buffered fetch head. Capture and hold them with existing instruction metadata while retaining flush/hold and operand-capture behavior.'},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Integrate exact balanced comparison, derive branch mismatch directly against saved expected primitive, separate jump recovery, retain exact architectural branch/trap/PC semantics, and export agreement plus saved training-allow eligibility through the unchanged EX-advance protocol.'},\n {'file':'rtl/branch_predictor.sv','description':'Extend the queued event with a saved training-allow bit; consume candidate events only when allowed. Retain all 64 counters, exact saturation/reset, folded indexing, stored-state lookup and existing update latency.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire the branch validator and queued training-filter interface while preserving existing recovery, skid, hazard, arithmetic and nret=1 top-level contracts.'},\n {'file':'test/test_branch_validate.py','description':'Check all six legal conditions and both prediction polarities against independent whole-word Python comparisons. Include equal operands, each nibble as the highest differing group, exhaustive four-bit primitive cases, signed/unsigned extrema and deterministic random operands.'},\n {'file':'test/test_branch_predictor.py','description':'Retain independent binary saturating-counter expectations and existing old-state, alias, saturation, simultaneous-event, hold and reset checks. Extend the model to candidate events with saved allow filtering and assert equivalence to the original legal/nontrapping training stream.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Retain every architectural/progress assertion, adapt bundle observers and source lists, and test all branch kinds/outcomes/predictions, reserved and misaligned encodings, delayed/filtered training, buffered fetch, both bus stalls and JAL/JALR recovery. Assert identical RVFI operands/PC/order, exactly-once training and effects, no wrong-path memory/arithmetic action, and unchanged DIV/REM retirement-gap bounds.'}\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=92,default_flow_style=False))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Schema valid:',p)\nprint('Category:',d['category'],'Expected:',d['expected_impact'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
